/* SRE & Cloud Deployment Foundations — lessons for M09–M12.

   A lesson: objective, prerequisites, 8–20 minute reading estimate, a
   mental model, 2–6 sections, one worked example, misconceptions, a short
   recall/prediction exercise (answer before commands), and references into
   the source register (LX.sreSources ids). Plain-language first, correct
   terms second. Markdown-lite: paragraphs, "- " bullets, `code`, **bold**,
   ``` blocks. Every example is the fictional Beacon service (Python HTTP
   API + worker + PostgreSQL + Redis) on invented hosts. */
window.LX = window.LX || { commands: [], scenarios: [], drills: [] };
LX.sreLessons = LX.sreLessons || [];

/* ═══ M09 · Kubernetes operations ═══════════════════════════════════ */
LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m09-01', moduleId: 'M09', title: 'Scheduling, requests and limits', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m09-01'], prereqs: ['les-m08-02'], refs: ['T02'],
  objective: 'Explain how the scheduler chooses a node from requests, taints and selectors, read a FailedScheduling event, and size requests and limits from evidence rather than by doubling.',
  model: 'A **request** is a reservation the scheduler books against a node\'s **allocatable** capacity; a **limit** is a ceiling the kubelet enforces at runtime. The scheduler never looks at real usage — only at the sum of requests already booked. It first **filters** nodes (enough unreserved CPU and memory, no untolerated **taint**, matching **nodeSelector**/affinity, volume and port constraints) and then **scores** the survivors. If the filter leaves zero nodes the Pod stays **Pending** and the reason is written into a `FailedScheduling` event, node by node. Requests decide *where* a Pod can go; limits decide *what happens* when it uses more: CPU is throttled, memory is killed.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Requests book capacity; limits enforce it', body: 'Every node advertises `Allocatable` CPU and memory (capacity minus what the kubelet and system reserve). When a Pod asks for `requests: {cpu: 250m, memory: 256Mi}`, the scheduler needs a node where `allocatable - sum(requests of Pods already there) >= 250m and 256Mi`. Actual usage is irrelevant to this arithmetic: a node whose Pods all request 1 CPU but idle at 2% is "full" once requests add up to its allocatable, and a node whose Pods request nothing is "empty" even while it is swapping.\n\nLimits are different. The kubelet turns `limits.cpu` into a CFS quota, so a container that wants more CPU than its limit is **throttled** (it runs slower; it is not killed). `limits.memory` becomes a cgroup memory ceiling, so a container that allocates past it is **OOM-killed** by the kernel (exit code 137, reason `OOMKilled`). You can see both numbers on a node with `kubectl describe node <name>` under `Allocated resources`, which prints requests and limits as a percentage of allocatable.' },
    { h: 'QoS classes: who dies first under node pressure', body: 'Kubernetes derives a **QoS class** from what you set:\n\n- **Guaranteed**: every container has requests equal to limits for both CPU and memory.\n- **Burstable**: at least one request or limit is set but they do not all match.\n- **BestEffort**: nothing set at all.\n\nWhen a *node* (not a container) runs short of memory, the kubelet evicts BestEffort Pods first, then Burstable Pods using more than they requested, and Guaranteed Pods last. That ordering is why `kubectl get pod -o jsonpath=\'{.status.qosClass}\'` is a useful question during a "random restarts" investigation. It is also why "set no requests so it can schedule anywhere" is a trap: a BestEffort Pod schedules everywhere and is the first thing thrown off a busy node.' },
    { h: 'Taints, tolerations and selectors are filters too', body: 'A taint on a node (`kubectl taint nodes w3 dedicated=db:NoSchedule`) says "do not place Pods here unless they tolerate this". `NoSchedule` affects new placement, `PreferNoSchedule` is a soft version, and `NoExecute` also evicts running Pods that lack a toleration. Control-plane nodes in most clusters carry `node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule`, which is why a single-node kind cluster removes it and a three-node one leaves it on.\n\nA `nodeSelector` or required node affinity works in the other direction: the Pod insists on a label the node must carry. Each of these is a separate filter and the `FailedScheduling` event reports them separately, so a message like `0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated taint {node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: }, 2 Insufficient memory` tells you exactly which filter rejected which node — and that fixing the taint alone would not help, because the two worker nodes failed on memory.' },
    { h: 'Sizing without guessing', body: 'The anti-pattern is to react to `Insufficient memory` by halving the request, or to react to an OOM kill by doubling the limit, and repeating until it stops. Both moves are made without evidence. The evidence is usage over time: `kubectl top pod -n beacon --containers` (which needs metrics-server) for a snapshot, and a Prometheus query such as `max_over_time(container_memory_working_set_bytes{container="api"}[1d])` for the peak. Set the memory request near the typical working set, the memory limit somewhat above the observed peak with headroom for growth, and the CPU request at the sustained need. Leave CPU limits off or generous unless you have a reason to throttle: a throttled API shows up as latency, not as a clean failure.\n\nThen reconcile with the cluster: if the sum of your requests cannot fit on the nodes you have, the honest answer is more or larger nodes or fewer replicas, not a request number chosen to make the scheduler happy.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'The Beacon worker that will not schedule after a "safety" change', body: 'A teammate raised the worker\'s memory request from `256Mi` to `4Gi` "to be safe". Afterwards:\n\n```\n$ kubectl get pod -n beacon\nNAME                             READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\nbeacon-worker-6d9f7c4b8-x2k7p    0/1     Pending   0          3m\n$ kubectl describe pod -n beacon beacon-worker-6d9f7c4b8-x2k7p | tail -3\n  Warning  FailedScheduling  12s (x4 over 3m)  default-scheduler  0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated taint {node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: }, 2 Insufficient memory. preemption: 0/3 nodes are available: 1 Preemption is not helpful for scheduling, 2 No preemption victims found for incoming pod.\n$ kubectl describe node looped-foundations-worker | grep -A4 "Allocated resources"\n  Resource           Requests      Limits\n  cpu                1150m (57%)   2 (100%)\n  memory             2560Mi (66%)  3Gi (79%)\n```\n\nReasoning: each worker node has roughly 3.8Gi allocatable and already has 2.5Gi booked, so no node has 4Gi free, and the control-plane node is tainted. Real usage from `kubectl top pod` over the last day peaked at 310Mi. The smallest evidence-based change is a request of `384Mi` and a limit of `512Mi`, which fits on either worker. Removing the taint would not have helped; neither would deleting other Pods to make room for a number nobody measured.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'The scheduler looks at how busy a node actually is.', right: 'It only sums requests against allocatable. A node can be "full" of idle Pods or "empty" and swapping; usage is the kubelet\'s and metrics-server\'s business, not the scheduler\'s.' },
    { wrong: 'Exceeding a CPU limit kills the container like exceeding a memory limit does.', right: 'CPU over the limit is throttled (slower, higher latency). Memory over the limit is OOM-killed. They fail differently and show up differently.' },
    { wrong: 'Leaving requests unset makes a Pod easier to run.', right: 'It makes it BestEffort: easiest to schedule and first to be evicted under node memory pressure, and invisible to the HPA.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: a 3-node cluster, every node has 1Gi memory free of requests but 3Gi free in real usage. A Pod requests 2Gi. Does it schedule?', a: 'No. The scheduler compares the request with unreserved allocatable (1Gi), not with real free memory. It stays Pending with Insufficient memory on all nodes.' },
    { q: 'A FailedScheduling event says `1 node(s) had untolerated taint ..., 2 Insufficient cpu`. Would adding a toleration fix it?', a: 'Not by itself: the toleration opens one node (the control-plane node), but the two workers failed on CPU. Fix the CPU request or capacity, and only tolerate the control-plane taint if running there is actually intended.' },
    { q: 'Which QoS class does a container with `requests.memory: 256Mi` and no other settings get, and why does that matter?', a: 'Burstable (a request exists but requests and limits do not all match). It is evicted after BestEffort Pods but before Guaranteed ones when the node is under memory pressure.' },
    { q: 'What command shows the requests and limits already booked on a node?', a: '`kubectl describe node <name>` — the Allocated resources table prints requests and limits as absolute values and as a percentage of allocatable.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m09-02', moduleId: 'M09', title: 'Pending, CrashLoopBackOff and OOMKilled evidence', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m09-01'], prereqs: ['les-m09-01', 'les-m08-05'], refs: ['T02', 'T22'],
  objective: 'Read a Pod\'s status, events, lastState and previous logs to separate "cannot be placed", "keeps exiting" and "was killed by the kernel", and name the exit code that distinguishes each before changing anything.',
  model: 'A Pod has a short life story and Kubernetes writes it down in three places. **Events** (`kubectl describe pod`) record what the scheduler and kubelet did and why. The **container status** records the current state and, crucially, the **lastState** of the previous run with its exit code and reason. The **previous log** (`kubectl logs --previous`) is what the container said before it died. `Pending` means the story has not started: no node, no image, or no volume. `CrashLoopBackOff` means the story keeps ending and the kubelet is waiting longer each time before retrying (10s, 20s, 40s… capped at 5 minutes). `OOMKilled` with exit code **137** means the kernel ended it; exit **143** means something asked it politely with SIGTERM; exit **1** means the program ended itself. Read the exit code before you read anything else.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Pending: it never started', body: 'A Pending Pod has no running containers because something before the container step failed. The events say which:\n\n- `FailedScheduling` with a node-by-node reason: resources, taints, selectors, or a volume that cannot be attached in this zone.\n- `FailedMount` or `FailedAttachVolume`: the node was chosen but a PVC is unbound or a ConfigMap/Secret named in the spec does not exist (`MountVolume.SetUp failed ... configmap "beacon-config" not found`).\n- No events at all after a scheduler assignment and status `ContainerCreating`: usually image pull. `ErrImagePull` and `ImagePullBackOff` appear in the container state, with the registry\'s message in the event (`manifest unknown`, `unauthorized`, or a DNS failure for `registry.lab`).\n\nThe discriminating question is "did it get a node?": `kubectl get pod -o wide` shows the NODE column empty for a scheduling failure and filled for everything after it.' },
    { h: 'CrashLoopBackOff: it starts, then exits', body: 'CrashLoopBackOff is not a cause; it is the kubelet saying "this container has exited N times and I am backing off before the next restart". The cause is in two places. First, the exit code and reason of the last run:\n\n```\n$ kubectl get pod -n beacon beacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-q8w2z -o jsonpath=\'{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState}\'\n{"terminated":{"exitCode":1,"reason":"Error","startedAt":"...","finishedAt":"..."}}\n```\n\nSecond, what it printed before exiting: `kubectl logs -n beacon beacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-q8w2z --previous`. Without `--previous` you get the *current* attempt, which may be a few lines of startup before the same failure — or nothing if it is still in backoff. Exit code 1 with a Python traceback is an application or configuration error. Exit code 0 with reason `Completed` and a Deployment (restartPolicy Always) is also a crash loop: a process that finishes its work and exits is wrong for a Deployment and right for a Job. A liveness probe that fails repeatedly also produces restarts, with the event `Liveness probe failed` and, usually, exit 143 if the process honoured SIGTERM or 137 if the kubelet had to SIGKILL after the grace period.' },
    { h: 'OOMKilled: the kernel ended it', body: 'When a container exceeds its memory limit the cgroup OOM killer terminates the process with SIGKILL. The kubelet records `reason: OOMKilled, exitCode: 137` in lastState, the Pod restarts, and the log often ends mid-line with no error at all — the process did not get a chance to write one. That absence is itself evidence: a crash with a traceback is the application; a crash with no final message and 137 is the kernel.\n\nTwo more things to check before raising the limit. `kubectl top pod --containers` shows whether usage climbs steadily (a leak or unbounded cache, which a bigger limit only delays) or sits near the limit from the start (a limit set below the real working set). And `kubectl describe node` under `Conditions` shows `MemoryPressure`, which means the *node* is short and the kubelet is evicting — a different mechanism, reported as `Evicted` in the Pod status rather than `OOMKilled` in the container.' },
    { h: 'The investigation order', body: 'For any unhealthy Pod, in this order and read-only:\n\n1. `kubectl get pod -n beacon -o wide` — status, restarts, node.\n2. `kubectl describe pod` — events at the bottom, container state and last state in the middle.\n3. `kubectl logs --previous` for a restarting container; `kubectl logs` for a running one.\n4. `kubectl top pod --containers` and, if the Pod is Pending, `kubectl describe node`.\n\nOnly then change one thing: the config key the traceback named, the request that could not be placed, the limit below the measured working set. Deleting the Pod to "give it a fresh start" destroys lastState and the previous log, the two things that would have told you the cause, and the ReplicaSet creates an identical Pod that fails identically.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Three Beacon API Pods, three different stories', body: '```\n$ kubectl get pod -n beacon\nNAME                          READY   STATUS             RESTARTS      AGE\nbeacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-q8w2z    0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   6 (2m ago)    9m\nbeacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-m4n1r    0/1     Pending            0             9m\nbeacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-z7t3c    0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (40s ago)   9m\n```\n\n`q8w2z`: lastState `{"terminated":{"exitCode":1,"reason":"Error"}}`; `kubectl logs --previous` ends with `KeyError: \'BEACON_DB_URL\'`. The container exits by itself because a required variable is missing: a configuration fault in the Deployment\'s env.\n\n`m4n1r`: no NODE in `-o wide`; events say `0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient memory`. It never ran; nothing in the logs will help.\n\n`z7t3c`: lastState `{"terminated":{"exitCode":137,"reason":"OOMKilled"}}`; `--previous` logs stop mid-request with no traceback; `kubectl top` shows 498Mi against a 512Mi limit within 30 seconds of start. The limit is below the working set.\n\nThree Pods of the same Deployment with three distinct failures is unusual but instructive: the env fault affects every replica, the memory fault depends on which node and which traffic a replica received, and the Pending one is capacity. One fix (set `BEACON_DB_URL`) will turn `q8w2z` healthy and leave the other two exactly as they are.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'CrashLoopBackOff is the error.', right: 'It is the restart policy at work. The error is the previous container\'s exit code, reason and last log lines; read those.' },
    { wrong: 'If `kubectl logs` shows nothing, there is no evidence.', right: 'An empty or truncated log with exit 137 is strong evidence of an OOM kill. Use `--previous`, `describe` for lastState, and `top` for usage.' },
    { wrong: 'Deleting a crashing Pod is a safe reset.', right: 'The ReplicaSet makes an identical one that fails the same way, and you have thrown away lastState and the previous log.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: a container has `lastState.terminated.exitCode: 143`. Which signal ended it, and what two things typically send it?', a: 'SIGTERM (128 + 15). The kubelet during a rollout, scale-down or failed liveness probe; or an operator deleting the Pod. It is not an OOM kill.' },
    { q: 'A Pod shows `Pending` for ten minutes. Which single column of `kubectl get pod -o wide` tells you whether to look at the scheduler or at volumes/images?', a: 'NODE. Empty means scheduling failed (read FailedScheduling); filled means it was placed and the problem is mounting or pulling after placement.' },
    { q: 'Why does `kubectl logs` without `--previous` often mislead during a crash loop?', a: 'It shows the current attempt, which may be a fresh start that has not failed yet, or nothing while the kubelet backs off. The failing run is the previous one.' },
    { q: 'Usage climbs from 100Mi to the 512Mi limit over 40 minutes, then OOMKilled, repeatedly. Is raising the limit to 1Gi a fix?', a: 'No — it doubles the time to the same kill. A steadily climbing working set points at unbounded growth in the app (a cache, a leak); the limit is only the place it becomes visible.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m09-03', moduleId: 'M09', title: 'Storage: PVCs and StorageClasses', mins: 13,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m09-02'], prereqs: ['les-m09-02'], refs: ['T02', 'T04'],
  objective: 'Bind a PersistentVolumeClaim through a StorageClass, explain why a claim is Pending under WaitForFirstConsumer, show data surviving a Pod replacement, and state what a local volume on a single-node cluster does not prove.',
  model: 'A **PersistentVolumeClaim** is a request ("give me 5Gi, read-write by one node"); a **PersistentVolume** is a piece of real storage; a **StorageClass** names the **provisioner** that turns claims into volumes and the policy for doing so. A claim is **Bound** once a PV is matched to it, and the Pod mounts the claim, never the PV directly. The class\'s **volumeBindingMode** decides *when* binding happens: `Immediate` on creation, `WaitForFirstConsumer` only when a Pod that uses the claim is scheduled, so the volume can be created where the Pod will run. The **reclaimPolicy** decides what happens to the data when the claim is deleted. The thing to keep in view: durability is a property of the *storage behind the PV*, not of the Kubernetes objects in front of it.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Claim, class, volume, mount', body: 'The Beacon PostgreSQL StatefulSet declares a `volumeClaimTemplates` entry; the controller creates one PVC per replica (`data-beacon-db-0`). The claim names a `storageClassName` (or omits it to take the cluster default, marked `(default)` in `kubectl get storageclass`) and an access mode: `ReadWriteOnce` (one node at a time), `ReadOnlyMany`, `ReadWriteMany` (needs a shared filesystem such as NFS; a local disk cannot do it), or `ReadWriteOncePod`. The provisioner creates a PV sized to the request and the two are bound:\n\n```\n$ kubectl get pvc -n beacon\nNAME               STATUS   VOLUME                                     CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS   AGE\ndata-beacon-db-0   Bound    pvc-5a1e...                                5Gi        RWO            standard       2m\n```\n\nThe Pod spec mounts the claim by name under `volumes:` → `persistentVolumeClaim.claimName`. If the claim is not Bound when the Pod is scheduled, the Pod sits in `ContainerCreating` with a `FailedMount` event; if the named claim does not exist, the event says so and nothing is created for you.' },
    { h: 'Pending claims: two very different reasons', body: 'With `volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer` a brand-new PVC shows `Pending` and `kubectl describe pvc` reports `waiting for first consumer to be created before binding`. That is normal: the class is waiting to learn which node the Pod lands on so it can create the volume there (local disks, zonal cloud disks). Create the Pod and both move to Bound together. Treating this as an error and "fixing" it by switching the class to `Immediate` can create a volume in a zone the Pod cannot be scheduled to.\n\nThe other Pending is real: `no persistent volumes available for this claim and no storage class is set` means there is no default class and the claim named none, so nobody is going to provision anything. Check `kubectl get storageclass` for a `(default)` marker, and if none, set `storageClassName` explicitly. A third variant, `ProvisioningFailed`, carries the provisioner\'s own message (quota, unsupported access mode, size too small) in the events.' },
    { h: 'Data survives the Pod, not necessarily the node', body: 'Delete the database Pod and the StatefulSet recreates `beacon-db-0`, which reattaches `data-beacon-db-0`; the rows are still there. This is the test the labs make you run and it proves one thing: the volume outlives the container. It does not prove the data would survive a lost node or zone.\n\nkind\'s default class `standard` uses a local-path provisioner: the PV is a directory on the node that happens to be a Docker container on your machine. The PV carries a node affinity, so the Pod is pinned to that node forever; if the node goes, the data goes. Cloud block volumes are zonal: they survive a node failure within the zone and are re-attached to a replacement node there, but not across zones. Only replication (database streaming replication, or a storage system that replicates) gives you survival of a zone, and that is a database topic, not a PVC setting. State this honestly in any report: "verified persistence across Pod replacement on a single-node local-path volume; durability across node or zone loss not demonstrated".' },
    { h: 'Reclaim policy and the cost of a tidy-up', body: 'The StorageClass sets `reclaimPolicy`, usually `Delete`: when the PVC is deleted the PV and its data are removed. `Retain` keeps the PV in `Released` state for manual recovery. Deleting a StatefulSet does not delete its PVCs by default, which is deliberate; deleting the *namespace* does. A `kubectl delete pvc` on a Delete-policy class is a data-destroying command and should be treated like `DROP DATABASE`: name the backup you would restore from before running it. Resizing is one-way (`allowVolumeExpansion: true` lets you grow a claim; nothing lets you shrink it), and expansion on a local-path volume is a no-op because the "volume" is a directory.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A Pending claim that was not a problem, and one that was', body: 'Two claims in the Beacon namespace after a fresh apply:\n\n```\n$ kubectl get pvc -n beacon\nNAME               STATUS    VOLUME   CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS   AGE\ndata-beacon-db-0   Pending                                      standard       40s\nbeacon-exports     Pending                                                     40s\n$ kubectl describe pvc -n beacon data-beacon-db-0 | tail -2\n  Normal  WaitForFirstConsumer  5s (x4 over 40s)  persistentvolume-controller  waiting for first consumer to be created before binding\n$ kubectl describe pvc -n beacon beacon-exports | tail -2\n  Normal  FailedBinding  5s (x4 over 40s)  persistentvolume-controller  no persistent volumes available for this claim and no storage class is set\n$ kubectl get storageclass\nNAME       PROVISIONER             RECLAIMPOLICY   VOLUMEBINDINGMODE      ALLOWVOLUMEEXPANSION   AGE\nstandard   rancher.io/local-path   Delete          WaitForFirstConsumer   false                  3d\n```\n\nThe first claim is waiting for the `beacon-db-0` Pod, which is itself waiting on an image pull; once the Pod schedules, the claim binds. No change needed. The second claim named no class and the cluster has no default (`standard` lacks the `(default)` marker), so it will wait forever. The fix is `storageClassName: standard` on the claim — not marking the class default on a shared cluster without agreement, and not hand-creating a hostPath PV to make the symptom go away.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A Pending PVC always means the storage is broken.', right: 'Under WaitForFirstConsumer it is waiting for a Pod to be scheduled; the describe output says so. The broken case names a missing class or a provisioner failure.' },
    { wrong: 'Data that survived a Pod restart is durable.', right: 'It survived the container. A local-path or hostPath volume dies with the node, and a zonal disk dies with the zone; only replication protects against those.' },
    { wrong: 'Deleting a Deployment or StatefulSet deletes the data.', right: 'PVCs outlive the workload by default. Deleting the PVC (or namespace) under a Delete reclaim policy is what destroys data.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: you create a PVC with the kind `standard` class and no Pod. What STATUS does `kubectl get pvc` show one minute later, and is that a fault?', a: 'Pending, with a WaitForFirstConsumer event. Not a fault: the class binds only when a consuming Pod is scheduled.' },
    { q: 'A Beacon database Pod on kind is deleted and recreated with its rows intact. Write the one-sentence limit statement that belongs in the evidence report.', a: 'Persistence across Pod replacement was verified on a single-node local-path volume; survival of node or zone loss was not demonstrated and requires replication.' },
    { q: 'Which access mode would a shared exports directory read by three API Pods on different nodes need, and why can a local disk not provide it?', a: 'ReadWriteMany. A local or zonal block disk attaches to one node at a time; RWX needs a network filesystem.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m09-04', moduleId: 'M09', title: 'RBAC and service accounts', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m09-04'], prereqs: ['les-m08-04'], refs: ['T02'],
  objective: 'Create a least-privilege Role and RoleBinding for a service account, read a Forbidden error back to the missing rule, and verify both with `kubectl auth can-i --as` and a real API call from inside the Pod.',
  model: 'Kubernetes authorisation is a list of **allow** rules and nothing else: there is no deny rule, and permissions are the union of every binding that names you. A **Role** is a set of rules (`apiGroups`, `resources`, `verbs`, optionally `resourceNames`) inside one namespace; a **ClusterRole** is the same shape but cluster-wide or for non-namespaced resources. A **RoleBinding** attaches a Role *or* a ClusterRole to subjects within one namespace; a **ClusterRoleBinding** attaches a ClusterRole everywhere. A Pod acts as its **ServiceAccount**, whose identity is `system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<name>`, using a short-lived token projected into the container. "Can this Pod list Pods?" is therefore a question about which bindings name its service account, and the API server will answer it for you before you deploy anything.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Rules: group, resource, verb', body: 'A rule is the triple of API group, resource and verb. The core group (Pods, Services, ConfigMaps, Secrets) is written as the empty string `""`; Deployments live in `apps`; NetworkPolicies in `networking.k8s.io`. Verbs are `get`, `list`, `watch`, `create`, `update`, `patch`, `delete`, `deletecollection`. A rule for `resources: ["pods"]` does not cover `pods/log` or `pods/exec` — subresources are named separately, which is exactly the point: a reader of Pod status should not get a shell.\n\n```\napiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata: { name: beacon-api-reader, namespace: beacon }\nrules:\n- apiGroups: [""]\n  resources: ["configmaps"]\n  resourceNames: ["beacon-config"]\n  verbs: ["get", "watch"]\n```\n\n`resourceNames` narrows to specific objects, with one catch: `list` cannot be restricted by name (a list has no name yet), so a Role that needs `list` grants it for every object of that kind in the namespace.' },
    { h: 'Bindings and service accounts', body: 'A RoleBinding\'s `subjects` can be a `User`, a `Group` or a `ServiceAccount` (with its namespace). `roleRef` names either a Role in the same namespace or a ClusterRole; a ClusterRole referenced from a RoleBinding is still scoped to that namespace, which is how the built-in `view`, `edit` and `admin` ClusterRoles are reused per namespace. `roleRef` is immutable: changing which role a binding points at means deleting and recreating the binding.\n\nEach Pod runs as `spec.serviceAccountName` (default: the namespace\'s `default` account). The kubelet projects a bound token into `/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token`, along with `ca.crt` and `namespace`; the token is audience-bound, time-limited and rotated, so copying it out of a Pod buys an attacker less than it used to, but it still carries every permission the account has. Two defaults to override deliberately: give every workload its own account (never bind rules to `default`), and set `automountServiceAccountToken: false` on Pods that make no API calls at all, so a compromised Beacon API container has no token to steal.' },
    { h: 'Verify before and after, from both sides', body: '`kubectl auth can-i` asks the API server the authorisation question without performing the action:\n\n```\n$ kubectl auth can-i get configmaps/beacon-config -n beacon --as=system:serviceaccount:beacon:beacon-api\nyes\n$ kubectl auth can-i list secrets -n beacon --as=system:serviceaccount:beacon:beacon-api\nno\n$ kubectl auth can-i --list -n beacon --as=system:serviceaccount:beacon:beacon-api\n```\n\n`--list` prints every rule that applies, which is the fastest way to notice an over-broad binding you did not write. Impersonation with `--as` needs you to hold `impersonate` permission, which cluster-admin has.\n\nThe second half of verification is a real call with the real token from inside the Pod, because `can-i` tests the rules and the real call tests the whole path (token mounted, CA trusted, Service reachable):\n\n```\n$ kubectl exec -n beacon deploy/beacon-api -- sh -c \'curl -sS --cacert /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)" https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/beacon/secrets\'\n```\n\nExpected for a least-privilege account: HTTP 403 with `secrets is forbidden: User "system:serviceaccount:beacon:beacon-api" cannot list resource "secrets" in API group "" in the namespace "beacon"`. A 401 is a different fault entirely: the token was not accepted at all (missing, expired, wrong audience).' },
    { h: 'Reading a Forbidden error back to the rule', body: 'The 403 message is a template: `<resource> is forbidden: User "<identity>" cannot <verb> resource "<resource>" in API group "<group>" in the namespace "<ns>"`. Every blank in it maps to a field in a rule. `cannot list resource "pods" in API group ""` needs `apiGroups: [""]`, `resources: ["pods"]`, `verbs: ["list"]`. `cannot get resource "deployments" in API group "apps"` needs `apps`. `at the cluster scope` instead of a namespace means a RoleBinding cannot help — the resource is cluster-scoped (nodes, namespaces, PVs) and needs a ClusterRoleBinding. Write the rule that the message names, nothing wider; then run `can-i` for that verb and for a verb you did *not* grant, so your evidence shows both the allow and a deny.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'The Beacon worker that "needs cluster-admin"', body: 'A pull request binds `cluster-admin` to the worker\'s service account "because it was getting Forbidden errors". The worker log shows one line: `configmaps "beacon-config" is forbidden: User "system:serviceaccount:beacon:beacon-worker" cannot get resource "configmaps" in API group "" in the namespace "beacon"`.\n\nThat one message is the whole specification. The least-privilege change:\n\n```\n$ kubectl create role beacon-worker-config -n beacon --verb=get,watch --resource=configmaps --resource-name=beacon-config\n$ kubectl create rolebinding beacon-worker-config -n beacon --role=beacon-worker-config --serviceaccount=beacon:beacon-worker\n$ kubectl auth can-i get configmaps/beacon-config -n beacon --as=system:serviceaccount:beacon:beacon-worker\nyes\n$ kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n beacon --as=system:serviceaccount:beacon:beacon-worker\nno\n```\n\nThen a restart of the worker (so it retries the read) and a real request from inside the Pod returning 200 for that ConfigMap and 403 for `secrets`. The review comment on the PR: the cluster-admin binding would have let any code running in the worker — including a dependency with a vulnerability — read every Secret in the cluster and delete nodes. The Forbidden message named one resource and one verb; the fix grants exactly those.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'RBAC can deny a permission another binding granted.', right: 'There are only allow rules; the effective permission is the union of all bindings. Removing access means removing or narrowing a binding, never adding a "deny".' },
    { wrong: 'A RoleBinding to a ClusterRole grants cluster-wide access.', right: 'The binding\'s namespace scopes it. That is the standard way to hand the built-in `view` or `edit` roles out per namespace.' },
    { wrong: '`auth can-i` saying yes proves the Pod can do it.', right: 'It proves the rules allow it. The Pod also needs the token mounted, the CA trusted and the API reachable; a real call from inside the container proves the whole path.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict the HTTP status and message when a service account with only `get configmaps` lists Secrets in its namespace with a valid token.', a: '403 Forbidden: `secrets is forbidden: User "system:serviceaccount:<ns>:<name>" cannot list resource "secrets" in API group "" in the namespace "<ns>"`. A 401 would mean the token itself was rejected.' },
    { q: 'An error says `cannot list resource "nodes" in API group "" at the cluster scope`. Will a Role plus RoleBinding fix it?', a: 'No. Nodes are cluster-scoped; it needs a ClusterRole bound with a ClusterRoleBinding (and the question of whether the workload should read nodes at all).' },
    { q: 'Why does the course forbid binding rules to the namespace\'s `default` service account?', a: 'Every Pod that does not name an account uses it, so any rule bound there leaks to every workload in the namespace, including ones that need no API access.' },
    { q: 'Which `kubectl auth can-i` flag shows every permission an identity holds in a namespace?', a: '`--list` (with `-n` and `--as=system:serviceaccount:ns:name`); it reveals over-broad bindings you did not write.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m09-05', moduleId: 'M09', title: 'NetworkPolicy and enforcement', mins: 16,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m09-03'], prereqs: ['les-m08-03', 'les-m09-04'], refs: ['T05', 'T04'],
  objective: 'Write a default-deny policy plus the allow rules Beacon needs (API to database, worker to Redis, DNS egress), prove both allowed and denied traffic from inside Pods, and state which CNI is enforcing the policy and how you know.',
  model: 'A **NetworkPolicy** is a namespaced object that **selects Pods** with a `podSelector` and lists what traffic those Pods may receive (**ingress**) and send (**egress**). The semantics have two halves. First: a Pod that is selected by *no* policy accepts and sends everything. Second: as soon as *any* policy with `policyTypes: [Ingress]` selects a Pod, that Pod accepts only what some policy explicitly allows — so an empty ingress list is **default deny**, and the same holds for Egress. Rules are **additive** across policies; there is no deny rule to write. And none of it does anything by itself: the API server stores the object, the **CNI plugin** enforces it, and kind\'s default `kindnet` does not. On a non-enforcing cluster a policy "applies" with no error and changes nothing.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Selecting Pods and declaring directions', body: '`spec.podSelector` chooses which Pods the policy governs; `{}` means every Pod in the namespace. `policyTypes` says which directions this policy speaks for. The classic namespace baseline:\n\n```\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata: { name: default-deny, namespace: beacon }\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]\n```\n\nNo `ingress:` or `egress:` lists, so every Pod in `beacon` is now isolated in both directions. Omitting `policyTypes` makes Kubernetes infer it: `Ingress` always, `Egress` only if an `egress` list is present — a common surprise where someone writes an egress-only policy without `policyTypes` and finds they also default-denied ingress. Write `policyTypes` explicitly every time.' },
    { h: 'Allow rules: peers, ports and the AND/OR trap', body: 'An allow rule names peers (`podSelector`, `namespaceSelector`, `ipBlock`) and ports. The database should accept only the API and the worker:\n\n```\nspec:\n  podSelector: { matchLabels: { app: beacon-db } }\n  policyTypes: [Ingress]\n  ingress:\n  - from:\n    - podSelector: { matchLabels: { app: beacon-api } }\n    - podSelector: { matchLabels: { app: beacon-worker } }\n    ports: [{ protocol: TCP, port: 5432 }]\n```\n\nTwo list items under `from` are **OR**: API or worker. A single item carrying *both* `namespaceSelector` and `podSelector` is **AND**: Pods with that label in namespaces with that label. Put a dash in the wrong place and "monitoring Pods in the monitoring namespace" becomes "any Pod in monitoring, plus any Pod anywhere with that label". Namespaces carry an automatic label `kubernetes.io/metadata.name: <name>`, which is the reliable way to select one by name. Policies are stateful: a reply to allowed traffic is allowed without an egress rule on the server side, so you reason about who *initiates* connections.' },
    { h: 'Egress default deny breaks DNS first', body: 'The first thing that fails after an egress default-deny is name resolution, because the Pod can no longer reach CoreDNS in `kube-system`. Every egress-restricted namespace needs an explicit DNS allowance, on both UDP and TCP 53 (large answers and some resolvers fall back to TCP):\n\n```\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [Egress]\n  egress:\n  - to:\n    - namespaceSelector: { matchLabels: { kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system } }\n      podSelector: { matchLabels: { k8s-app: kube-dns } }\n    ports:\n    - { protocol: UDP, port: 53 }\n    - { protocol: TCP, port: 53 }\n```\n\nThen the specific egress each workload needs: API → `beacon-db` on 5432 and `beacon-redis` on 6379; worker → the same plus, if it posts to an external endpoint, an `ipBlock` for that address range. Symptom check: a denied DNS lookup shows as `could not resolve host` after a timeout; a denied TCP connection shows as a **timeout**, not `connection refused` — refused means a packet arrived and nothing was listening, which a policy drop never produces.' },
    { h: 'Enforcement is a property of the CNI, and you must prove it', body: 'NetworkPolicy objects are data. The API server validates and stores them regardless of whether anything will enforce them. kind clusters created with the default CNI (`kindnet`) accept policies and ignore them; the labs use a kind config with `networking.disableDefaultCNI: true` and install Calico (Cilium also enforces). Before trusting any policy, prove enforcement with a negative test:\n\n```\n$ kubectl run -n beacon probe --rm -it --restart=Never --image=registry.lab/tools/netshoot:2025.10 -- sh\n~ $ timeout 5 nc -zv beacon-db 5432; echo exit=$?\nnc: connect to beacon-db (10.96.14.2) port 5432 (tcp) timed out\nexit=1\n```\n\nA probe Pod *without* the `app: beacon-api` label must time out; a Pod *with* it must connect (`beacon-db (10.96.14.2:5432) open`). If the unlabelled probe connects, either the policy does not select what you think (`kubectl describe networkpolicy` shows the selectors and rules as the API parsed them) or nothing is enforcing. An evidence report lists the CNI (`kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=calico-node`) alongside the allow and deny results; "the policy applied cleanly" on its own is not evidence of anything.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Segmenting Beacon and finding out the first policy did nothing', body: 'A learner applied `default-deny` and the database allow rule to a kind cluster and reported "segmentation done". Checking:\n\n```\n$ kubectl get networkpolicy -n beacon\nNAME           POD-SELECTOR     AGE\ndefault-deny   <none>           5m\ndb-allow       app=beacon-db    5m\n$ kubectl run -n beacon probe --rm -it --restart=Never --image=registry.lab/tools/netshoot:2025.10 -- nc -zv beacon-db 5432\nbeacon-db (10.96.14.2:5432) open\n$ kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o name | grep -E \'calico|cilium\'\n(no output)\n$ kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l app=kindnet -o name\npod/kindnet-9fz2x\n```\n\nThe unlabelled probe connected: the policies exist and nothing enforces them. After recreating the cluster with the policy profile (default CNI disabled, Calico installed), the same probe times out, a probe labelled `app: beacon-api` connects, and — new symptom — the API Pod logs `could not translate host name "beacon-db" to address`. The egress default-deny cut DNS; adding the kube-dns egress rule restores resolution, and the API connects on 5432 while the worker still cannot reach the API on 8080 (it has no rule to, and should not). The report records: CNI Calico, allow proven (labelled probe, API to DB), deny proven (unlabelled probe timeout, worker to API timeout), DNS egress rule required.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'If `kubectl apply` accepted the NetworkPolicy, traffic is now restricted.', right: 'The API stores policies; only a CNI that implements them enforces them. kind\'s default kindnet does not. Prove it with a connection that should fail.' },
    { wrong: 'A policy with no rules allows everything.', right: 'Selecting a Pod with `policyTypes: [Ingress]` and no ingress list isolates it completely: that is how default deny is written.' },
    { wrong: 'Two selectors in one `from` item means "either".', right: 'One item with both namespaceSelector and podSelector is AND; separate items are OR. The dash placement changes the meaning.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict the symptom when an egress default-deny is applied to the `beacon` namespace with no other rule: what does the API log when it starts?', a: 'A DNS failure (`could not translate host name "beacon-db"` or similar) after a timeout, because egress to CoreDNS in kube-system on UDP/TCP 53 is now dropped.' },
    { q: 'A denied connection — does it fail with `connection refused` or a timeout, and why does the distinction matter?', a: 'Timeout. Policy enforcement drops packets silently; "refused" means the packet arrived and nothing listened, so refused points at the service, not the policy.' },
    { q: 'You apply a policy on a fresh kind cluster with default networking. What one test tells you whether it is doing anything?', a: 'Connect from a Pod the policy should block (an unlabelled probe to beacon-db:5432). If it succeeds, nothing is enforcing: kindnet does not implement NetworkPolicy.' },
    { q: 'Write, in words, the rule that lets Prometheus in namespace `monitoring` scrape Beacon API Pods on 9102 and nothing else.', a: 'Ingress on Pods labelled app=beacon-api, from one item combining namespaceSelector kubernetes.io/metadata.name=monitoring AND podSelector app=prometheus, ports TCP 9102.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m09-06', moduleId: 'M09', title: 'Disruption budgets, drains and scaling', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m09-05', 'obj-m09-06'], prereqs: ['les-m09-01'], refs: ['T02', 'T01'],
  objective: 'Explain what a PodDisruptionBudget protects, perform a drain and explain why a budget blocked it instead of bypassing it, list what an HPA needs before it can scale, and tell adding replicas apart from removing a bottleneck.',
  model: 'A **drain** is a *voluntary* disruption: you ask the Eviction API to move Pods off a node, and the API consults each Pod\'s **PodDisruptionBudget** before saying yes. A PDB states, for a set of Pods, the `minAvailable` or `maxUnavailable` at any moment, and the API refuses an eviction that would breach it; the drain retries until it can proceed. The budget is the application telling the cluster its tolerance; a blocked drain is that tolerance working. **Horizontal scaling** is a separate loop: the **HPA** reads metrics (through metrics-server) and compares usage to *requests*, adding replicas when the ratio is high. More replicas help only when the replicas themselves are the constraint; if the constraint is a shared dependency, more replicas add load to the thing that was already saturated.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Budgets: what the application can tolerate', body: '```\napiVersion: policy/v1\nkind: PodDisruptionBudget\nmetadata: { name: beacon-api, namespace: beacon }\nspec:\n  minAvailable: 2\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: beacon-api } }\n```\n\nWith three healthy API replicas, one eviction at a time is allowed; with two, none is. `kubectl get pdb -n beacon` prints `MIN AVAILABLE`, `ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS` and the current count, which is the first thing to read when a drain hangs. Budgets only govern *voluntary* disruptions (drain, eviction API, some autoscaler scale-downs); a node crash, an OOM kill or a `kubectl delete pod` ignore them completely. Two shapes that bite: `minAvailable: 1` on a single-replica Deployment makes the node undrainable by design, and a selector that matches nothing protects nothing while looking configured. `maxUnavailable` is usually the better choice for Deployments because it scales with the replica count.' },
    { h: 'Draining a node and reading a blocked drain', body: '`kubectl drain looped-foundations-worker2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data` first cordons the node (`SchedulingDisabled`, so nothing new lands) and then evicts each Pod through the Eviction API. DaemonSet Pods are skipped with the flag because they would be recreated on the same node; `--delete-emptydir-data` is an admission that emptyDir contents are disposable. A blocked drain looks like this and keeps retrying:\n\n```\nevicting pod beacon/beacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-q8w2z\nerror when evicting pods/"beacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-q8w2z" -n "beacon" (will retry after 5s): Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod\'s disruption budget.\n```\n\nThe right response is to find out *why* the budget has no room: `kubectl get pdb` shows `ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS: 0`, and `kubectl get pod -l app=beacon-api -o wide` usually shows that only two replicas are Ready — because one is still CrashLooping from an earlier incident, or because two replicas sit on this very node. Fix the readiness, or scale up temporarily so the budget has slack, then let the drain continue. `--disable-eviction` (which deletes instead of evicting) and `--force` bypass the budget, and bypassing a budget is choosing an outage the application owner said they could not take.' },
    { h: 'The HPA and its prerequisites', body: 'A HorizontalPodAutoscaler targets a Deployment and a metric: `averageUtilization: 60` of CPU means "keep the average usage across replicas at 60% of each Pod\'s CPU *request*". Three things must be true before it does anything:\n\n- **metrics-server** (or another `metrics.k8s.io` provider) is installed and healthy; kind does not ship it. Without it `kubectl get hpa` shows `TARGETS: <unknown>/60%` and the events say `FailedGetResourceMetric`.\n- The containers have **requests** for the metric you target; utilisation is a percentage of the request, so with no request the HPA has nothing to divide by.\n- `minReplicas`/`maxReplicas` bound it, and the Deployment\'s `replicas` field is left to the HPA (a GitOps-managed manifest that pins `replicas: 2` will fight it).\n\nThe loop checks every 15 seconds and scales down with a stabilisation window, so a spike ends before the scale-up lands and a scale-down lags by minutes; the HPA smooths capacity, it does not absorb bursts. Its own events (`SuccessfulRescale`, `New size: 5; reason: cpu resource utilization above target`) are the record of what it did and why.' },
    { h: 'More replicas is not the same as more capacity', body: 'Scaling out helps when the API Pods are the saturated resource: CPU throttled, request queues full, latency rising with load on the Pods themselves. It does nothing — or harm — when the saturated resource is shared. Beacon has one PostgreSQL with `max_connections = 100` and each API replica opens a pool of 10. At 10 replicas the pool fits; the HPA scales to 12 and two replicas fail with `FATAL: sorry, too many clients already` while latency stays high because the database CPU was the real limit. The USE check for the dependency (utilisation, saturation, errors on the database and Redis) tells you which case you are in before you scale.\n\nThe habit: name the bottleneck with a measurement, then pick the lever that moves it. Replicas for Pod-local saturation; an index, a pooler, caching or a larger database for a saturated dependency; and in a burst, backpressure (reject early with a clear status) rather than ever-more replicas that convert a queue into a crash.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A drain that hung and the scale-up that made it worse', body: 'A maintenance runbook step: drain `worker2`. It hangs with `Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod\'s disruption budget`. Evidence:\n\n```\n$ kubectl get pdb -n beacon\nNAME         MIN AVAILABLE   MAX UNAVAILABLE   ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS   AGE\nbeacon-api   2               N/A               0                     30d\n$ kubectl get pod -n beacon -l app=beacon-api -o wide\nNAME                          READY   STATUS             NODE\nbeacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-a1b2c    1/1     Running            worker\nbeacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-d3e4f    1/1     Running            worker2\nbeacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-g5h6i    0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   worker\n```\n\nOnly two replicas are Ready, so evicting the one on `worker2` would leave one: the budget is right to refuse. The crash-looping Pod (a leftover from the morning\'s bad config) is the real blocker; fixing it restores three Ready and the drain proceeds. The teammate\'s instinct — `kubectl scale deploy beacon-api --replicas=6` — would have worked too, but the HPA later did that on its own during the afternoon peak and the database hit its connection ceiling: six replicas × a pool of 20 exceeded `max_connections = 100`. Both episodes had the same lesson. The budget and the connection limit were each a dependency stating its tolerance; the fix was to respect the statement, not to push past it.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A blocked drain means the PDB is misconfigured.', right: 'Usually it means fewer Pods are healthy than the application needs; the budget is doing its job. Find out why there is no slack before touching the budget.' },
    { wrong: 'An HPA works as soon as it is created.', right: 'It needs metrics-server (or another metrics API), resource requests on the containers, and the Deployment\'s replica count left to it. Otherwise TARGETS shows unknown.' },
    { wrong: 'If latency is high, add replicas.', right: 'Only if the replicas are the saturated resource. If a shared database or cache is saturated, more replicas add pressure and can trigger connection-limit errors.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: a Deployment has 1 replica and a PDB with `minAvailable: 1`. What happens to `kubectl drain` on its node?', a: 'It hangs retrying every few seconds: any eviction would violate the budget. Either scale to 2 first or accept the downtime explicitly; do not force-delete.' },
    { q: '`kubectl get hpa` shows `<unknown>/60%`. Name the two most likely causes in order of checking.', a: 'metrics-server missing or unhealthy (check `kubectl top pod` and the HPA events for FailedGetResourceMetric), then missing CPU requests on the target containers.' },
    { q: 'Beacon API latency doubled; CPU on the Pods is 20% and `beacon_db_query_duration_seconds` p95 went from 5 ms to 200 ms. Does scaling the API help?', a: 'No. The dependency is slow; more replicas send more concurrent queries to an already-slow database. Investigate the database (locks, plan, saturation) instead.' },
    { q: 'Which disruptions does a PDB not protect against?', a: 'Involuntary ones: node crashes, kernel OOM kills, kubelet eviction under pressure, and direct `kubectl delete pod`. It only gates the Eviction API (drain, autoscaler scale-down).' }
  ]
});

/* ═══ M10 · Helm, CI/CD and GitOps ══════════════════════════════════ */
LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m10-01', moduleId: 'M10', title: 'Helm values, templates and chart versions', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m10-01'], prereqs: ['les-m08-04', 'les-m09-06'], refs: ['T13'],
  objective: 'Render, lint, install, upgrade and roll back the Beacon chart, explain the precedence of values and the difference between chart version and appVersion, and read a release history to say which revision is live and why.',
  model: 'A **chart** is a directory of templates plus a `values.yaml` of defaults; `helm template` turns them into plain Kubernetes manifests with no cluster involved. A **release** is one named installation of a chart in a namespace, and every install, upgrade or rollback adds a **revision** to its history, each storing the rendered manifest and the values that produced it. `Chart.yaml` carries two independent numbers: **version** is the chart\'s own SemVer (bump it whenever a template or default changes) and **appVersion** is the version of the software it deploys (informational, often the image tag). Values merge in a fixed **precedence**: the chart\'s defaults are lowest, then each `-f` file in order, then `--set`, which always wins. Helm manages manifests; it knows nothing about your database.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Templates and values', body: 'A template is YAML with Go-template holes: `image: {{ .Values.image.repository }}@{{ .Values.image.digest }}`, `replicas: {{ .Values.api.replicas }}`, and helpers from `_helpers.tpl` such as `{{ include "beacon.fullname" . }}` for consistent names and labels. `values.yaml` holds the defaults; an environment file holds what differs:\n\n```\n# values-staging.yaml\napi:\n  replicas: 2\n  resources: { requests: { cpu: 250m, memory: 256Mi }, limits: { memory: 512Mi } }\nimage:\n  repository: registry.lab/beacon/api\n  digest: sha256:9f3c...\n```\n\nRender without a cluster to see exactly what would be applied: `helm template beacon ./charts/beacon -n beacon -f values-staging.yaml | less`. Pipe it through `kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f -` to catch schema and admission errors before an upgrade touches anything. `helm lint ./charts/beacon -f values-staging.yaml` catches template errors, missing required values and chart-metadata problems; it does not know whether the values make operational sense.' },
    { h: 'Precedence, and the --reuse-values trap', body: 'Values merge, lowest to highest: `values.yaml` in the chart → a parent chart\'s values for a subchart → each `-f file` left to right (later files override earlier keys) → `--set key=value`. Maps merge key by key; a list is replaced whole, so overriding one element of `env:` replaces the entire list. `helm get values beacon -n beacon` prints the user-supplied values of the live revision; `--all` includes computed defaults.\n\n`helm upgrade --reuse-values` takes the *previous revision\'s* values as the base instead of the chart\'s defaults. It is tempting for a quick `--set` change and dangerous after a chart upgrade, because new defaults in the new chart are ignored and a stale value from months ago silently persists. The course convention: always pass the full `-f` file for the environment, never rely on reuse, and keep that file in Git.' },
    { h: 'Install, upgrade, history, rollback', body: '`helm upgrade --install beacon ./charts/beacon -n beacon --create-namespace -f values-staging.yaml --wait --timeout 5m` is idempotent: it installs on first run and upgrades after. `--wait` blocks until Pods are Ready (per their readiness probes) and marks the release `failed` if they are not within the timeout; `--atomic` additionally rolls back automatically on failure. The history is the record:\n\n```\n$ helm history beacon -n beacon\nREVISION  UPDATED                   STATUS      CHART         APP VERSION  DESCRIPTION\n1         Mon Oct  6 09:12:03 2026  superseded  beacon-1.3.0  2.4.1        Install complete\n2         Thu Oct  9 14:40:51 2026  superseded  beacon-1.4.0  2.5.0        Upgrade complete\n3         Mon Oct 13 10:02:17 2026  failed      beacon-1.4.1  2.5.1        Upgrade "beacon" failed: timed out waiting for the condition\n4         Mon Oct 13 10:09:40 2026  deployed    beacon-1.4.0  2.5.0        Rollback to 2\n```\n\n`helm rollback beacon 2 -n beacon` does not delete revision 3; it creates revision 4 whose manifest is revision 2\'s. The live state is always the single `deployed` row. The chart version tells you which templates are live; the app version tells you which Beacon is running — and note that revision 3\'s image may have been fine while the chart change (a wrong probe path, say) caused the failure.' },
    { h: 'What a rollback does not do', body: 'Rollback re-applies manifests. Kubernetes then performs a normal rolling update back to the old Pod template, honouring readiness and the disruption budget. It does not undo anything the new version *did*: rows it wrote, a schema migration it ran, messages it published in a new format, a Secret it rotated. A chart that runs migrations in a pre-upgrade hook needs a stated compatibility rule (the next lesson on expand/contract) or every rollback is a gamble. Helm hooks, `--wait` and `history` give you a clean release record; whether the application tolerates going backwards is yours to prove.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Reading a failed upgrade without guessing', body: 'A staging upgrade timed out. Before rolling back:\n\n```\n$ helm history beacon -n beacon | tail -2\n2  ...  superseded  beacon-1.4.0  2.5.0  Upgrade complete\n3  ...  failed      beacon-1.4.1  2.5.1  Upgrade "beacon" failed: timed out waiting for the condition\n$ kubectl get pod -n beacon -l app=beacon-api\nbeacon-api-5f8b9c7d6-k2m4n   0/1   Running   0   6m   # new\nbeacon-api-7c9d4d5f6-a1b2c   1/1   Running   0   4d   # old, still serving\n$ kubectl describe pod -n beacon beacon-api-5f8b9c7d6-k2m4n | grep -A2 Readiness\n  Readiness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 404\n$ helm get manifest beacon -n beacon --revision 3 | grep -A3 readinessProbe\n      readinessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /ready, port: http }\n```\n\nChart 1.4.1 renamed the probe path to `/ready`; app 2.5.1 still serves `/readyz`. The old ReplicaSet kept serving because the rollout never passed readiness, so users saw nothing. `helm rollback beacon 2 -n beacon` cleans up the record (revision 4, deployed), and the fix is a one-line chart change plus a chart version bump to 1.4.2 — not a `--set` on the next upgrade that would have to be remembered forever.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Bumping appVersion is the same as bumping the chart version.', right: 'They are independent. `version` is the chart\'s SemVer and must change when templates or defaults change; `appVersion` describes the software and is informational.' },
    { wrong: 'A rollback removes the failed revision.', right: 'It adds a new revision whose manifest copies the target. History is append-only; only the `deployed` row is live.' },
    { wrong: 'A `--set` on the command line is a quick, harmless override.', right: 'It has the highest precedence and lives nowhere in Git, so the next upgrade from the values file silently reverts it — or, with --reuse-values, silently keeps it forever.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: chart default `api.replicas: 1`, `values-staging.yaml` says 2, and the command adds `--set api.replicas=3`. What is rendered?', a: '3. The order is chart defaults < -f files < --set; --set always wins.' },
    { q: 'History shows revisions 1 (superseded), 2 (superseded), 3 (failed), 4 (deployed, "Rollback to 2"). Which manifest is live and which chart version?', a: 'Revision 4, which is a copy of revision 2\'s manifest and chart version (the CHART column of row 4 shows it).' },
    { q: 'Which command shows the manifests an upgrade would apply, without a cluster?', a: '`helm template <release> <chart> -f <values>`; add `helm lint` for template errors and `kubectl apply --dry-run=server` for schema and admission checks.' },
    { q: 'After `helm rollback`, is the database schema the previous version\'s?', a: 'No. Rollback re-applies manifests only; whatever migrations or writes the new version performed remain. Compatibility must be designed in.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m10-02', moduleId: 'M10', title: 'Pipelines: tests, artifacts and gates', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m10-02'], prereqs: ['les-m04-04', 'les-m07-05', 'les-m10-01'], refs: ['T13', 'T06'],
  objective: 'Author a tests → build → scan → publish → promote pipeline in which a failing test or scan blocks publication, with a local script that runs the same gates so the pipeline is a thin wrapper around it.',
  model: 'A pipeline is a directed set of **stages** that each produce or consume **artifacts** and either pass or fail. A **gate** is a stage whose failure stops everything downstream: nothing is published if tests fail, nothing is promoted if the scan finds a blocking vulnerability. The artifacts are the thing being delivered (an image, identified by digest) plus the evidence about it (test report, scan report, SBOM). The pipeline runner is not where logic should live: every stage calls a **local executable equivalent** (`make test`, `./ci/scan.sh`) so a developer can run the same gate on a laptop, a failure can be reproduced without pushing, and the pipeline definition stays a thin list of calls in order.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Stages and what flows between them', body: 'Beacon\'s delivery path, in order:\n\n1. **test**: unit and contract tests (`pytest -q --junitxml=reports/junit.xml`), lint, type checks. Output: a report artifact. Nothing else runs if this fails.\n2. **build**: `docker buildx build --platform linux/arm64,linux/amd64 --tag registry.lab/beacon/api:2.5.1 --push .` with the commit SHA as a label. Output: the image and its **digest**, written to a small file (`build/digest.txt`) that later stages read.\n3. **scan**: an image scanner over the pushed digest plus an SBOM. Output: findings as JSON; the gate fails on unfixed CRITICAL findings or on a policy you wrote down.\n4. **publish**: the digest is recorded as a release candidate (a tag such as `2.5.1` and a Git-tracked file naming the digest). Only reached on the main branch, only after the gates.\n5. **promote**: the same digest is written into the staging values file, then the production one, each behind a review. The artifact never changes identity along the way.\n\nThe stages pass only the digest between them, never "the image I built a minute ago"; a later stage that rebuilds has produced a different artifact than the one that was tested.' },
    { h: 'Gates fail loudly and block, or they are decoration', body: 'A gate is only a gate if a non-zero exit stops the next stage. Three ways teams quietly disable their own gates: `|| true` after a flaky command, `continue-on-error`, and "retry until green" on a test that fails one run in five. The last one is the most expensive because it trains everyone to ignore red. A flaky test is a bug (a timing assumption, shared state, an unmocked network call) and is fixed or quarantined with a ticket, not retried.\n\nThe scan gate needs a written policy because scanners always find something: for Beacon, block on CRITICAL with a fix available; report HIGH; ignore findings in packages the image does not execute only with a documented justification file in the repo. An approval step before production is also a gate — a human one — and it belongs *after* every automated gate, so the approver sees a candidate that already passed, not a candidate that might.' },
    { h: 'The local equivalent', body: 'Every stage in the runner calls a script that works on a laptop:\n\n```\n# Makefile\ntest:  ; python -m pytest -q --junitxml=reports/junit.xml\nbuild: ; ./ci/build.sh $(VERSION)       # prints the digest to build/digest.txt\nscan:  ; ./ci/scan.sh $$(cat build/digest.txt)\nci: test build scan\n```\n\nThe pipeline file then reads like a table of contents (`run: make test`, `run: make build`, `run: make scan`) with `needs:` edges between the jobs and the digest file declared as an artifact of `build` consumed by `scan`. When the runner says a stage failed, the engineer runs the same `make` target locally with the same pinned tool versions and sees the same failure. Pipelines that embed shell inline in YAML drift from what anyone can run, and their failures become "works on my machine" in reverse.' },
    { h: 'Reading a pipeline failure', body: 'Treat a red pipeline the way you treat a failing Pod: find the stage, read its output, reproduce. The runner\'s summary tells you which stage; the stage log carries the tool\'s output (`FAILED tests/test_ingest.py::test_duplicate_key - AssertionError`, or `CRITICAL CVE-... in openssl 3.0.x fixed in 3.0.y`). A scan failure after a *dependency* bump is usually a real finding; a scan failure with no code change is usually the vulnerability database updating, which means the base image needs a rebuild — a legitimate block, not noise. In both cases the artifact that failed is identified by digest in the log, so the evidence can be attached to a ticket exactly.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A green build that should have been red', body: 'Beacon\'s pipeline had this step:\n\n```\n- name: test\n  run: python -m pytest -q || echo "tests flaky, continuing"\n```\n\nIt produced images for two weeks while `test_duplicate_key` failed on every run. The failure was real: a regression in the ingest handler stored duplicate sensor records, and it reached staging because "test" was decoration. The repair had three parts: remove the `|| echo`, so the stage exits non-zero; make the `build` job depend on `test` with `needs: [test]`, so a failure actually blocks; and add `make ci` so the engineer could run the same three gates locally and watch the test go red in 40 seconds instead of waiting for the runner. The first run after the fix:\n\n```\ntest   FAILED  1 failed, 212 passed in 38.2s\nbuild  skipped (needs: test)\nscan   skipped\n```\n\nNo image was published, which is the gate doing its job. The regression was fixed on a branch, the test passed, and the next main-branch run produced a digest that went through scan and publish with the junit report and scan JSON stored next to it.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A pipeline that runs tests has a test gate.', right: 'Only if a failure stops publication. `|| true`, continue-on-error and automatic retries turn the stage into a report nobody reads.' },
    { wrong: 'The scan can be skipped when only code changed, not dependencies.', right: 'The vulnerability database changes daily; the same base image can become a blocking finding overnight. Scanning the pushed digest every time is the point.' },
    { wrong: 'Pipeline logic belongs in the pipeline YAML.', right: 'It belongs in scripts the YAML calls, so the same gate runs locally, failures reproduce, and the YAML stays a readable list of stages.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: the build stage runs `docker build` and the publish stage runs `docker build` again before pushing. What is wrong?', a: 'The published artifact is not the one that was tested and scanned; a rebuild can pick up different layers. Build once, pass the digest forward.' },
    { q: 'A test fails one run in five. Which of these is the gate-preserving response: retry on failure, mark it allowed-to-fail, or fix/quarantine with a ticket?', a: 'Fix or quarantine with a ticket. Retry and allowed-to-fail both train the team to ignore red and let a real regression through.' },
    { q: 'Where in the order does the human approval for production belong, and why?', a: 'After every automated gate (test, build, scan, publish), so the approver sees a candidate that already passed rather than one that might fail later.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m10-03', moduleId: 'M10', title: 'Immutable promotion by digest and rollback', mins: 13,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m10-03'], prereqs: ['les-m07-05', 'les-m10-02'], refs: ['T06', 'T21'],
  objective: 'Promote one image digest from staging to production without rebuilding or retagging it into something different, verify from the running Pods that the intended digest is live, and roll back to a previous digest without changing any artifact\'s identity.',
  model: 'An OCI image has two kinds of name. A **tag** (`2.5.1`, `staging`) is a mutable pointer the registry lets anyone move. A **digest** (`sha256:9f3c…`) is the content hash of the manifest: it cannot be moved, forged or quietly replaced, and the same bytes always have the same digest. **Promotion** means the digest that passed the gates is referenced in the next environment\'s desired state; the bytes do not change, only who points at them. **Rollback** means pointing at the previous digest, which still exists in the registry. Tags are for humans to find things; digests are what deployments reference, and what evidence records.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Why tags cannot carry a promise', body: 'If the staging values say `image: registry.lab/beacon/api:2.5.1`, three things can go wrong with no error anywhere: someone re-pushes `2.5.1` with a hotfix and staging silently runs different code than was scanned; the node already has a `2.5.1` cached and runs the old bytes (depending on `imagePullPolicy`); or production is deployed a week later from the same tag and gets whichever bytes are there now. A digest reference, `registry.lab/beacon/api@sha256:9f3c…`, has none of these failure modes: the registry serves exactly those bytes or an error, and the kubelet verifies the hash. The course rule follows: tags in build logs and release notes, digests in every manifest and values file, and never a floating tag such as `latest`, `stable` or `prod` in a deployment.' },
    { h: 'Capturing and promoting the digest', body: 'The build stage captures the digest at push time:\n\n```\n$ docker buildx build --platform linux/arm64,linux/amd64 -t registry.lab/beacon/api:2.5.1 --push --metadata-file build/meta.json .\n$ jq -r \'."containerimage.digest"\' build/meta.json\nsha256:9f3c1a0b7e2d4c5f6a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f\n$ docker buildx imagetools inspect registry.lab/beacon/api:2.5.1 | head -3\nName:      registry.lab/beacon/api:2.5.1\nMediaType: application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json\nDigest:    sha256:9f3c1a0b...\n```\n\nFor a multi-platform image the digest is the *index*; per-architecture manifests sit below it, which is why one digest serves both ARM64 and AMD64 nodes. Promotion is a Git change: `deploy/staging/values.yaml` gets `image.digest: sha256:9f3c…`; after staging verification, the same line goes into `deploy/prod/values.yaml` in a reviewed change. Adding a convenience tag (`imagetools create -t registry.lab/beacon/api:prod registry.lab/beacon/api@sha256:9f3c…`) is fine for discovery; it adds a pointer and changes nothing about the artifact.' },
    { h: 'Verify from the running Pods', body: 'The desired state saying a digest is not the same as the digest running. Ask the kubelet:\n\n```\n$ kubectl get pod -n beacon -l app=beacon-api -o jsonpath=\'{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\\t"}{.status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}{"\\n"}{end}\'\nbeacon-api-5f8b9c7d6-k2m4n  registry.lab/beacon/api@sha256:9f3c1a0b...\nbeacon-api-5f8b9c7d6-p9q8r  registry.lab/beacon/api@sha256:9f3c1a0b...\n```\n\n`imageID` is what was actually pulled. Record it with the scan report for the same digest, and you have a line of evidence an auditor can follow from commit to running container. If `imageID` shows a different digest than the values file, the rollout is not finished, a Pod is from an old ReplicaSet, or something else is writing the Deployment — all worth knowing before declaring a promotion complete.' },
    { h: 'Rollback by digest', body: 'Rolling back is the same Git change in reverse: put the previous digest back in the values file and let the normal deployment path apply it. Because the previous image was never deleted or overwritten, it is still exactly the bytes that ran before. Two emergency shortcuts exist and both must be followed by the Git change: `helm rollback` to the previous revision (which contains the previous digest) and `kubectl rollout undo deployment/beacon-api`. Neither changes the artifact; both change the cluster without changing the source of truth, which the GitOps lesson shows is a temporary state.\n\nWhat a digest cannot promise is that the *previous code* still works against the *current data*: a destructive migration, a new message format in Redis, or a renamed config key breaks the rollback even though the bytes are identical. The compatibility check is a separate, deliberate step, and it comes before the rollback, not after.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A promotion that changed the artifact without anyone noticing', body: 'A release note said "promoted 2.5.1 to production". The scan report attached to the ticket was for `sha256:9f3c…`. Checking production:\n\n```\n$ kubectl get pod -n beacon -l app=beacon-api -o jsonpath=\'{.items[0].status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}\'\nregistry.lab/beacon/api@sha256:4b71d2e0...\n$ docker buildx imagetools inspect registry.lab/beacon/api:2.5.1 | grep Digest\nDigest:    sha256:4b71d2e0...\n```\n\nBetween staging and production an engineer had rebuilt `2.5.1` to "include the logging fix" and re-pushed the tag. Production ran bytes nobody had scanned, and the evidence trail pointed at a different image. The repair: values files reference `image.digest`, the chart renders `@sha256:` references, the pipeline writes the digest into the promotion PR, and a check job fails if the tag\'s current digest differs from the one in the PR. The rebuild would then have been a new candidate with its own scan, not a silent swap.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A version tag like 2.5.1 is immutable.', right: 'Tags are pointers the registry lets anyone move; only the digest is content-addressed. Reference digests in manifests, use tags for discovery.' },
    { wrong: 'Promotion means rebuilding for the next environment.', right: 'Rebuilding produces a different artifact than the one that passed the gates. Promotion re-references the same digest.' },
    { wrong: 'If the previous digest is deployed again, the system is back to how it was.', right: 'The code is identical; the data, schema and queued messages are whatever the newer version left. Compatibility is a separate check.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: staging and production both reference `registry.lab/beacon/api:2.5.1`. Someone re-pushes that tag. What do the two environments run after their next Pod restart?', a: 'Whatever bytes the tag points at now, possibly different in each depending on node image caches and pull policy. With digest references both keep running the scanned image.' },
    { q: 'Which field proves what a Pod is actually running, as opposed to what its spec asked for?', a: '`.status.containerStatuses[].imageID`, which carries the pulled digest.' },
    { q: 'Why does a multi-platform image have one digest that serves both ARM64 and AMD64?', a: 'The digest identifies the image index; the index lists per-architecture manifests and the node picks the matching one.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m10-04', moduleId: 'M10', title: 'GitOps reconciliation', mins: 13,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m10-04'], prereqs: ['les-m10-01', 'les-m10-03'], refs: ['T13', 'T12'],
  objective: 'Explain why an emergency `kubectl` change disappears under a reconciler, make the same change safely through the source of truth, and when a pause is justified, suspend reconciliation as a documented, time-boxed step and resume it.',
  model: 'GitOps makes a Git repository the **desired state** and runs a **reconciler** in the cluster that repeatedly compares what Git says with what the cluster has and applies the difference. Nothing in the loop knows or cares that a human typed `kubectl scale` ten minutes ago: that is **drift**, and the next reconcile removes it. Flux and Argo CD are two implementations of this loop; the course uses them as examples, not requirements. Because the loop is the mechanism, there are exactly two legitimate ways to make a change: change Git (the normal path, with the fast lane of a reviewed emergency commit), or **suspend** the loop for a stated reason and time, make the change, and resume after Git has caught up. An undocumented pause is a reconciler someone forgot to turn back on.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'The loop, and what drift looks like', body: 'A Flux `Kustomization` or an Argo `Application` names a repository, a path and an interval. Every interval (often one to ten minutes) the controller renders the path (plain manifests, Kustomize or a Helm chart with values) and applies it with server-side apply, pruning objects that left Git. Argo additionally reports `OutOfSync` when live differs from Git and, with `selfHeal` enabled, reverts the difference without waiting for the interval.\n\nThe canonical drift episode: the Beacon API is overloaded, an engineer runs `kubectl scale deploy/beacon-api -n beacon --replicas=6`, latency recovers, and four minutes later the Deployment is back to `replicas: 2` and latency is bad again. `kubectl get deploy` shows the change, `kubectl describe deploy` shows a `ScalingReplicaSet` event from the reconciler\'s identity, and `flux get kustomizations` or `argocd app get beacon` shows a successful sync at that moment. Nothing failed; the system did what it is built to do.' },
    { h: 'Change the source of truth', body: 'The safe version of the same emergency is a small reviewed commit: edit `deploy/prod/values.yaml` (`api.replicas: 6`), open a change that one other person can approve in minutes, merge, and either wait for the interval or trigger the reconcile (`flux reconcile kustomization beacon --with-source`, `argocd app sync beacon`). Two properties make this the default even at 03:00: the change is recorded with who and why, and it will still be true after the next reconcile. If the HPA owns the replica count, the right commit raises `maxReplicas` or the HPA target rather than the Deployment, because a reconciler and an HPA both writing `replicas` is a fight you lose twice.' },
    { h: 'A documented pause', body: 'Sometimes Git cannot carry the change yet: the fix is a live experiment (a one-off env flag to capture a trace), or the pipeline that updates Git is itself broken. Then pause reconciliation explicitly for that object:\n\n```\n$ flux suspend kustomization beacon -n flux-system\n► suspending kustomization beacon in flux-system namespace\n✔ kustomization suspended\n# or: argocd app set beacon --sync-policy none\n```\n\nand write down, in the incident channel or ticket: what was paused, by whom, why, what manual change follows, and the resume condition and deadline. Make the change, observe, then either commit the equivalent to Git and `flux resume kustomization beacon` (the reconciler applies Git, which now matches), or revert the manual change by hand and resume. A pause without an owner and a deadline is the most common way a GitOps cluster ends up with months of silent drift and a repository nobody trusts.' },
    { h: 'What to check when "GitOps did not apply my change"', body: 'The loop can fail in the other direction too. The reconciler reports its own status: `flux get kustomizations` shows `READY False` with the reason (a template error, a dependency not ready, a path that does not exist after a rename); `kubectl describe kustomization beacon -n flux-system` carries the event with the exact apply error (`dry-run failed: ... Deployment.apps "beacon-api" is invalid`). Argo shows the same under the app\'s sync result. A commit on a branch the Kustomization does not watch, or a values file change the chart does not read, produces no apply at all and no error; the evidence is a last-applied revision that does not match your commit SHA.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'The scale-up that kept vanishing', body: 'Incident timeline, Beacon production:\n\n- 09:41 latency alert fires; on-call runs `kubectl scale deploy/beacon-api --replicas=6`. Latency recovers.\n- 09:46 alert fires again. `kubectl get deploy beacon-api` shows `2/2`. On-call scales again.\n- 09:51 same. `kubectl describe deploy beacon-api | grep -A3 Events` shows `ScalingReplicaSet ... from 6 to 2` attributed to the Flux kustomize-controller; `flux get kustomizations` shows `Applied revision: main@sha1:3f9e...` at 09:50.\n\nOn-call recognises the loop and takes the pause path because the pipeline is mid-outage: `flux suspend kustomization beacon`, scale to 6, and posts in the channel: "Suspended Flux kustomization `beacon` at 09:53 (me) to hold replicas=6 during DB incident; resume by 12:00 or when PR #412 (replicas 6 in values) merges." PR #412 merges at 10:20; `flux resume kustomization beacon` applies it; the Deployment stays at 6 because Git now says 6; the channel note is updated. The postmortem action item: the HPA, not a fixed replica count, should own this Deployment\'s replicas, so the next incident needs no pause at all.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'The reconciler reverting my fix is a bug in the reconciler.', right: 'It is the defining behaviour: the cluster converges to Git. Change Git, or pause the loop deliberately and briefly.' },
    { wrong: 'Suspending reconciliation is an admin convenience that needs no record.', right: 'A silent pause is how clusters drift for months. The pause is recorded with owner, reason and resume condition, and resumed when Git matches.' },
    { wrong: 'If Git has the change, the cluster has the change.', right: 'Only if the reconciler watches that branch and path, the render succeeds and the apply is accepted. Check the reconciler\'s own status and last-applied revision.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: Flux reconciles every 5 minutes and you run `kubectl set env deploy/beacon-api LOG_LEVEL=debug`. What is the state 10 minutes later?', a: 'The env change is gone, reverted at the next reconcile; events on the Deployment show the controller\'s apply. The same is true for any manual field the manifest in Git specifies.' },
    { q: 'An emergency needs 6 replicas now and the CI pipeline is down. Name the two acceptable paths.', a: 'A direct reviewed commit to the deploy repo (merge without waiting for CI if policy allows), or a documented, time-boxed `suspend` of that object followed by the manual change, a Git catch-up commit and `resume`.' },
    { q: 'A merged commit changed `values-prod.yaml`, nothing happened, no error. Where do you look?', a: 'The reconciler\'s status: watched branch and path, last applied revision versus the commit SHA, and whether the chart actually reads that values file.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m10-05', moduleId: 'M10', title: 'Secrets in delivery and schema compatibility', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m10-05'], prereqs: ['les-m10-03', 'les-m08-04'], refs: ['T13', 'T09'],
  objective: 'Keep secrets out of charts, values files, rendered output and logs while still delivering them to Pods, and recognise from a migration history when rolling back the application would be unsafe.',
  model: 'Two things travel with every release that the artifact does not contain: **secrets** and **schema**. Secrets must reach the Pod without ever being in Git, in a rendered manifest someone pasted into a ticket, or in a log line; the delivery pipeline carries a *reference* to a secret and the cluster resolves it. Schema changes are the reason a rollback can fail even when the previous image is byte-identical: code version N-1 expects the tables and columns it was written against. The **expand/contract** pattern keeps rollback safe by splitting every change into an additive step that both versions tolerate, a code deployment, and a destructive step that runs only after the rollback window has closed. A **destructive migration that has already run is never rolled back**; the only way forward is forward.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Where secrets leak in a delivery path', body: 'A Kubernetes Secret is base64-encoded, not encrypted; anyone who can read the object reads the value. Beyond the cluster, the common leaks are mundane: `values-prod.yaml` with `db.password: ...` committed "temporarily"; `helm template` output attached to a pull request; `helm get values --all` pasted into a chat; a CI job that `echo`es its environment; and the application itself logging its config at startup. Each of these puts the value where it will never be rotated out of.\n\nThe rules the Beacon chart follows: the chart never renders a Secret *value*, only references (`secretKeyRef`) to a Secret created outside the chart; environment values files contain the Secret\'s *name*, never its content; CI jobs receive secrets from the runner\'s secret store as masked variables and never print them; and the application logs which config keys were loaded, never their values. `kubectl describe pod` shows `BEACON_DB_URL: <set to the key \'url\' in secret \'beacon-db\'>` for a reference and the literal string for an inline value, which makes the review check easy.' },
    { h: 'Delivering a secret without carrying it', body: 'Two workable patterns at this level:\n\n- **Out-of-band creation plus reference**: an operator or a one-time bootstrap creates `Secret/beacon-db` in the namespace from the secret store; the chart only references it. Simple and auditable, and rotation is a `kubectl create secret ... --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -` plus a rollout.\n- **Encrypted-in-Git or external-secrets**: the repository stores either an encrypted blob only the cluster can decrypt or a pointer that an in-cluster controller resolves against a secret manager. GitOps-friendly because the desired state is complete without the plaintext ever being in Git.\n\nEither way the evidence is the same: `git log -p` over the repo shows no value, the rendered manifest shows a reference, and the Pod can connect. If a value does get committed, treat it as leaked: rotate first, scrub history second.' },
    { h: 'Expand, migrate code, contract', body: 'Renaming `sensor_status.state` to `status` the naive way is one migration: `ALTER TABLE sensor_status RENAME COLUMN state TO status`. Version N code works; version N-1 code, if rolled back, fails every query with `column "state" does not exist`. The rollback is impossible the moment the migration ran.\n\nThe compatible way takes three releases:\n\n1. **Expand** (release N): add `status` as a nullable column, backfill it, and ship code that writes both columns and reads `status` with a fallback to `state`. N-1 still works: it ignores the new column.\n2. **Switch** (release N+1): code reads and writes only `status`. Rollback to N works: N still writes both.\n3. **Contract** (release N+2, after the rollback window): `ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN state`. Rollback to N+1 works; rollback to N would break, and that is acceptable because N is outside the window.\n\nEach migration is forward-only and idempotent, recorded in a versions table (`schema_migrations`), and the release notes state the oldest code version the current schema supports. The chart\'s pre-upgrade migration hook runs the expand step; the contract step is a separate, deliberately scheduled change.' },
    { h: 'Recognising an unsafe rollback', body: 'Before any rollback, read two things: the migration history since the target version and the compatibility note. `SELECT version, applied_at FROM schema_migrations ORDER BY applied_at DESC LIMIT 5` tells you what ran; the migration files tell you whether any of it was destructive (DROP, RENAME, NOT NULL without default, type change). If a destructive step has run, the rollback target must be a version that tolerates the new shape — which may mean the rollback is a *forward* fix instead. "We can always roll back" is only true for teams whose migrations are all expansions inside the window; everyone else has to check.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'A rollback request that would have taken production down', body: 'Release 2.6.0 of Beacon is misbehaving (slow ingest). The on-call proposes `helm rollback beacon 7` to 2.5.1. Before running it:\n\n```\n$ psql -d beacon -c "select version, applied_at from schema_migrations order by applied_at desc limit 3"\n version | applied_at\n---------+---------------------\n 0043    | 2026-10-13 08:02:11   -- drop column sensor_status.state\n 0042    | 2026-10-06 09:15:40   -- add sensor_status.status, backfill\n 0041    | 2026-09-29 10:11:02\n$ git show v2.5.1:beacon/db/queries.py | grep -n "state"\n57:    "SELECT id, state, recorded_at FROM sensor_status WHERE ..."\n```\n\nMigration 0043 was the contract step for the column rename, and 2.5.1 still reads `state`. A rollback would deploy an image whose first query fails on every request: an outage instead of a slowdown. The team keeps 2.6.0 running, finds the ingest regression (an N+1 query introduced in the rename), ships 2.6.1 forward within the hour, and adds an action item: the contract migration ran one week after expand, inside the stated two-week rollback window, so the policy was violated and the window needs to be enforced by the pipeline, not by memory.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A Kubernetes Secret keeps the value safe.', right: 'It is base64, readable by anyone with get on secrets. Safety comes from RBAC, not storing it in Git, and never rendering or logging the value.' },
    { wrong: 'If the previous image is still in the registry, we can roll back.', right: 'The image is unchanged; the schema may not be. After a destructive migration the previous code can fail on its first query.' },
    { wrong: 'Down migrations make rollback safe.', right: 'A down migration that recreates a dropped column cannot recover the dropped data. Forward-only expand/contract avoids needing one.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: migration 0042 added a column and 0043 dropped the old one yesterday; code N-1 reads the old column. What happens on rollback to N-1?', a: 'Every query touching that column fails with `column does not exist`: a full outage. The rollback is unsafe; fix forward.' },
    { q: 'What does `kubectl describe pod` show for an env var sourced from a Secret, and why does that matter in review?', a: '`<set to the key \'x\' in secret \'y\'>` rather than the value. A literal string there means the value is in a manifest or values file, which means it is probably in Git.' },
    { q: 'In expand/contract, after which release is the old column dropped, and what decides the timing?', a: 'After the switch release has been live for the full rollback window; the window, not convenience, decides when contract runs.' }
  ]
});

/* ═══ M11 · Observability and SLOs ══════════════════════════════════ */
LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m11-01', moduleId: 'M11', title: 'Logs, metrics and traces: what each answers', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m11-01'], prereqs: ['les-m04-03', 'les-m08-03'], refs: ['T07', 'T14'],
  objective: 'Add Prometheus metrics, structured logs and trace context to Beacon, propagate a request id and trace context from the API through Redis to the worker, and follow one request across all three signals.',
  model: 'The three signals answer three different questions. **Metrics** are numbers aggregated over time — cheap to store for years, perfect for "how many, how fast, how often", useless for "which one". **Logs** are events with context — expensive per byte, perfect for "what exactly happened to request 7f3a", useless for computing a rate across a million of them. **Traces** are the causal tree of one request as it crosses processes — spans with parent ids and timings, perfect for "where did the 800 ms go", useless on their own for alerting. The glue between them is **correlation**: a **request id** and a **trace context** that every log line carries and every outbound call forwards, so a slow point found in metrics leads to a trace, and the trace leads to the exact log lines.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Metrics: counters, gauges, histograms', body: 'Prometheus scrapes an HTTP endpoint (`/metrics`) and stores every series it finds. In Beacon the API exposes three instruments with `prometheus_client`:\n\n```\nREQS = Counter("beacon_http_requests_total", "Requests", ["method", "route", "status"])\nLAT  = Histogram("beacon_http_request_duration_seconds", "Latency", ["route"],\n                 buckets=(0.025, 0.05, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2.5, 5))\nINFL = Gauge("beacon_http_requests_in_flight", "In-flight requests")\n```\n\nA **counter** only goes up (restarts reset it to zero; `rate()` handles that). A **gauge** is a current value. A **histogram** is a set of counters per bucket (`_bucket{le="0.25"}`) plus `_sum` and `_count`. The `route` label is the *template* (`/v1/sensors/{id}`), never the raw path; a raw path with an id in it makes one series per sensor and is the cardinality failure the M11-03 lesson is about. Metrics can tell you that p95 latency on `/v1/status` rose from 60 ms to 900 ms at 10:14; they cannot tell you which request or why.' },
    { h: 'Logs: structured, one event per line, with ids', body: 'A log line is for a human or a search engine, so it must be parseable and must carry the keys that let you join it to the other signals. Beacon logs JSON, one object per line, to stdout (the container runtime collects it; `kubectl logs` shows it):\n\n```\n{"ts":"2026-10-13T10:14:07.412Z","level":"info","msg":"status accepted","request_id":"7f3a9c","trace_id":"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736","span_id":"00f067aa0ba902b7","route":"/v1/status","sensor_id":"S-0042","db_ms":812}\n```\n\nThe fields `request_id`, `trace_id` and `span_id` are set once per request in middleware and attached to every log call for that request (a context variable, not a global). The high-cardinality detail — `sensor_id`, the exact duration — belongs here, where it costs one line, rather than in a metric label where it costs a series. Never log the payload body or a credential; log what identifies the event.' },
    { h: 'Traces: spans, parents and propagation', body: 'A trace is a tree of **spans**, each with a name, start and end time, attributes and a parent span id. The API creates a root span for the request, a child span for the database call, and a child for the Redis enqueue. The worker, in a different process, continues the *same trace* when it consumes the message — but only if the context travelled with the message. Over HTTP the carrier is the W3C `traceparent` header (`00-<trace-id>-<parent-span-id>-01`); over a queue the API writes the same string into the message payload (`"traceparent": "00-4bf9...-00f0...-01"`) and the worker extracts it before starting its span. Forget the extraction and you get two unrelated traces and a gap exactly where the queue delay you wanted to measure sits. Propagation is the whole difficulty of tracing; instrumentation libraries do the rest.' },
    { h: 'Following one request across the three', body: 'The pattern, from the outside in:\n\n1. **Metrics** show *that* something is wrong and *where in aggregate*: p95 for `/v1/status` is up; `beacon_db_query_duration_seconds` p95 is up; CPU is flat. So the database path, broadly.\n2. **Traces** show *where the time goes* in one slow request: root span 900 ms, child `db.query SELECT ... sensor_status` 812 ms, child `redis.lpush` 2 ms. So one query.\n3. **Logs**, filtered by that `trace_id` or `request_id`, show the *exact* event: the query text, the sensor id, the row count, the connection pool wait.\n\nEach signal narrows the next search; none replaces the others. Without the ids, step 2 to 3 is a guess over thousands of lines; without the route label, step 1 is "something is slow".' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'One slow status write, end to end', body: 'A user reports that submitting a sensor status "sometimes takes a second". Metrics: `histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(beacon_http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{route="/v1/status"}[5m])))` returns 0.91 s against a usual 0.06 s, starting 10:12. The DB histogram shows the same shape; CPU and in-flight requests do not. Traces: filtering for `/v1/status` with duration > 500 ms shows every slow trace has a `db.query` span of 700–900 ms, and the span attribute `db.statement` is the sensor-history lookup. Logs: `kubectl logs -n beacon deploy/beacon-api | grep 4bf92f35` returns the request\'s lines, including `{"msg":"pool wait","wait_ms":640,"pool_in_use":10,"pool_size":10}` — the time is not in the query itself but waiting for a connection, because every connection is busy with the same slow lookup. The worker log for the same trace id shows the enqueue arrived 1.2 s after the user\'s request began, which the trace would have shown as a gap had the `traceparent` not been copied into the message. Three signals, one story, and the fix (M12-01: an index on `sensor_status (sensor_id, recorded_at)`) is now a hypothesis with evidence rather than a guess.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'Logs with enough detail make metrics unnecessary.', right: 'Computing a rate or a percentile from logs is slow and expensive and gets slower as traffic grows; metrics pre-aggregate so questions about rates are instant and cheap for years.' },
    { wrong: 'A trace id in the API logs is enough for correlation.', right: 'Only if it is propagated: forwarded in headers to every callee and copied into queue messages. Otherwise the worker\'s work is a separate trace.' },
    { wrong: 'Put the sensor id on the metric so we can graph per sensor.', right: 'That creates one series per sensor and overwhelms Prometheus. Per-entity detail belongs in logs and trace attributes; metrics carry bounded labels.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Which signal answers "how many status writes failed in the last five minutes", and which answers "why did request 7f3a9c fail"?', a: 'Metrics (a counter with a status label and rate()) for the first; logs filtered by request_id (or the trace) for the second.' },
    { q: 'Predict: the worker starts a new span without reading `traceparent` from the message. What does the trace of a status write look like?', a: 'It ends at the Redis enqueue; the worker\'s processing appears as a separate root trace with no link, and the queue wait is invisible.' },
    { q: 'Name three fields every Beacon log line carries and the one kind of value that must never appear.', a: 'request_id, trace_id (with span_id) and the route/event name; never a credential or token, and not the raw payload body.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m11-02', moduleId: 'M11', title: 'RED, USE and PromQL basics', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m11-02'], prereqs: ['les-m11-01', 'les-m09-01'], refs: ['T01', 'T14'],
  objective: 'Use RED signals for Beacon and USE signals for its resources, write `rate()`, `sum by` and ratio queries in PromQL, and distinguish a slow dependency from local CPU saturation from the numbers.',
  model: 'Two small checklists cover most of what a dashboard should show. **RED** is for a service: **Rate** (requests per second), **Errors** (failed requests per second, or as a fraction), **Duration** (how long they take, as a distribution). **USE** is for a resource: **Utilisation** (how busy), **Saturation** (how much work is queued because it is busy), **Errors**. A latency problem is either the service waiting on a saturated *resource it owns* (CPU, pool, memory) or waiting on a *dependency* whose own RED has gone bad; the two look identical from the outside and different in these numbers. PromQL turns counters into rates with `rate()` over a window and collapses labels with `sum by (...)`; everything else is arithmetic on the results.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'rate, sum by, and why the window matters', body: 'Counters go up forever; what you want is the per-second rate over a recent window:\n\n```\nrate(beacon_http_requests_total[5m])                      # one series per label combination\nsum by (route) (rate(beacon_http_requests_total[5m]))     # one per route\nsum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total{status=~"5.."}[5m]))\n  / sum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total[5m]))              # error ratio, 0..1\n```\n\n`rate()` needs at least two samples inside the window, so with a 15 s scrape a `[1m]` window is fragile and `[5m]` is the usual floor; a longer window is smoother and slower to react. `rate()` is only meaningful on counters (it accounts for resets); applied to a gauge it produces nonsense. `increase()` is `rate()` times the window, for "how many in the last hour". `sum by (route)` keeps only the `route` label and adds everything else together; `sum without (instance)` is the complement. Always aggregate *after* `rate()`, never before: the sum of two counters that reset at different times is not a counter.' },
    { h: 'RED for Beacon', body: 'Rate: `sum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total[5m]))`, optionally by route. Errors: the ratio above, or `sum by (route) (rate(beacon_http_requests_total{status=~"5.."}[5m]))` to see which route fails. Duration: a percentile from the histogram (next lesson) or, for a quick look, `sum(rate(beacon_http_request_duration_seconds_sum[5m])) / sum(rate(beacon_http_request_duration_seconds_count[5m]))` for the mean. The same three for each dependency as seen *from Beacon*: `beacon_db_queries_total`, `beacon_db_query_duration_seconds`, `beacon_redis_commands_total`. Instrumenting the client side of a dependency is what lets you say "the database is slow *for us*" even when the database\'s own dashboard is green.' },
    { h: 'USE for the resources under Beacon', body: 'CPU utilisation of the API container: `sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{namespace="beacon", container="api"}[5m]))` in cores; divide by the request or limit for a fraction. CPU *saturation* is throttling: `rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total{container="api"}[5m]) / rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total{container="api"}[5m])` — a value near 0.5 means the container wanted CPU and was denied half the time, which shows up as latency with nothing in the logs. Memory: `container_memory_working_set_bytes` against the limit. The connection pool, instrumented by the app: `beacon_db_pool_in_use` against `beacon_db_pool_size` (utilisation) and `beacon_db_pool_wait_seconds` (saturation). Errors are the counters you already have.' },
    { h: 'Telling the two latency stories apart', body: 'Latency rose. Ask, in this order:\n\n1. Did request rate change? (`sum(rate(..._total[5m]))`) A step up points at load; flat points at something that got slower.\n2. Is the API CPU saturated? Throttling ratio up, usage at the limit → local saturation; more replicas or a higher limit is a candidate fix.\n3. Is a dependency slower? `beacon_db_query_duration_seconds` p95 up with API CPU flat → the database (or the network to it); pool wait rising with query time is the same story one step further.\n4. Is the pool the bottleneck even though queries are fast? `pool_in_use == pool_size` and wait high while query p95 is normal → concurrency, not the database.\n\nEach branch leads to a different lever, and only one of them is "add replicas". A dashboard that shows API latency alone cannot tell the stories apart; one that shows the RED of the service, the RED of each dependency as the client sees it, and the USE of CPU and pool, can.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Slow API, idle CPU', body: 'Alert: p95 latency on `/v1/status` above 500 ms for 10 minutes. The on-call runs four queries (results at 10:20):\n\n```\nsum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total[5m]))                                  → 212/s (usual 200/s)\nrate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total{container="api"}[5m])\n  / rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total{container="api"}[5m])             → 0.01\nhistogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(beacon_db_query_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])))\n                                                                            → 0.74 (usual 0.006)\nbeacon_db_pool_in_use / beacon_db_pool_size                                 → 1.0\n```\n\nRate is normal, so this is not load. CPU throttling is 1%, so the API is not saturated locally. Database query time rose a hundredfold and the pool is fully used because each connection is held for 740 ms instead of 6. The dependency is the story; scaling the API would add pool pressure on the database and fix nothing. The next step is on the database side — `pg_stat_activity` for lock waits or a plan change on the status lookup (M12) — and the alert that fired on user latency was correct to fire.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: '`rate()` works on any metric.', right: 'Only counters. On a gauge it computes a meaningless slope; use the gauge directly or `delta()`/`deriv()` if you really want its change.' },
    { wrong: 'High API latency with low CPU means the network is slow.', right: 'It means the API is waiting on something: a dependency, a pool, a lock. The client-side dependency metrics say which; the network is one hypothesis among several.' },
    { wrong: 'Averages are fine for latency.', right: 'The mean hides a slow tail that hits a minority of users every time. Percentiles from the histogram show it.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict the result shape of `sum by (route) (rate(beacon_http_requests_total[5m]))` versus `sum(rate(...))`.', a: 'The first gives one series per distinct route value; the second gives a single series with every label removed.' },
    { q: 'Write the PromQL for the fraction of Beacon requests that returned 5xx over the last five minutes.', a: '`sum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total{status=~"5.."}[5m])) / sum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total[5m]))`.' },
    { q: 'CPU throttling ratio 0.6, DB p95 unchanged, pool wait zero. Dependency or local saturation?', a: 'Local CPU saturation: the container is denied CPU 60% of periods; raise the limit or add replicas, after checking what the CPU is spent on.' },
    { q: 'Why aggregate after `rate()` rather than before?', a: 'Counters reset on restart at different times per instance; summing raw counters produces a series that is not a counter, and rate() on it is wrong.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m11-03', moduleId: 'M11', title: 'Histograms, latency and cardinality', mins: 14,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m11-05', 'obj-m11-02'], prereqs: ['les-m11-02'], refs: ['T14'],
  objective: 'Compute a latency percentile from a Prometheus histogram with `histogram_quantile`, explain why bucket boundaries limit its precision, identify a label that is exploding series count, and bound it without losing the signal you needed.',
  model: 'A Prometheus **histogram** is not a list of latencies; it is a row of **cumulative buckets** — counters of "how many observations were ≤ this boundary" — plus a sum and a count. `histogram_quantile(0.95, …)` finds the bucket where the 95th percentile falls and **interpolates linearly inside it**, so the answer is only as precise as the bucket edges you chose. Every distinct combination of label values is a **series**, and Prometheus pays memory and query time per series, not per sample. A label whose values are unbounded (user ids, sensor ids, raw paths, request ids) multiplies the series count without limit; that is **cardinality explosion**, and it is the most common way a monitoring system is taken down by the service it monitors.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'What a histogram stores', body: 'For `beacon_http_request_duration_seconds` with buckets `0.025, 0.05, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2.5, 5`, one scrape of one route produces:\n\n```\n..._bucket{route="/v1/status",le="0.025"} 8120\n..._bucket{route="/v1/status",le="0.05"}  9410\n..._bucket{route="/v1/status",le="0.1"}   9630\n..._bucket{route="/v1/status",le="0.25"}  9700\n..._bucket{route="/v1/status",le="0.5"}   9712\n..._bucket{route="/v1/status",le="1"}     9718\n..._bucket{route="/v1/status",le="2.5"}   9720\n..._bucket{route="/v1/status",le="5"}     9720\n..._bucket{route="/v1/status",le="+Inf"}  9720\n..._sum{route="/v1/status"}  412.7\n..._count{route="/v1/status"} 9720\n```\n\nBuckets are cumulative: `le="0.05"` counts everything at or under 50 ms, including the 8120 under 25 ms. `+Inf` always equals `_count`. The mean is `_sum / _count` (42 ms here) and it says nothing about the 8 requests over one second.' },
    { h: 'histogram_quantile and its precision', body: '```\nhistogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(beacon_http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{route="/v1/status"}[5m])))\n```\n\nThe `le` label must survive the aggregation — `sum by (le)` or `sum by (le, route)` — because the function needs the bucket boundaries to do anything. The 95th percentile of 9720 observations is the 9234th; that falls in the `le="0.05"` bucket (8120 < 9234 ≤ 9410), and the function reports a point linearly interpolated between 25 ms and 50 ms, about 46 ms. The true value could be anywhere in that range; a bucket layout with no edge between 50 ms and 100 ms cannot tell 60 ms from 95 ms. Choose edges around the values you make decisions on (your SLO threshold must be a bucket boundary, or the latency SLI is an estimate), and remember that a percentile over a tiny rate is noise.' },
    { h: 'Cardinality: how series multiply', body: 'Series count is the product of label values. `beacon_http_requests_total` with 12 routes × 5 methods × 8 status codes × 3 replicas is at most 1,440 series, fine. Add `sensor_id` with 40,000 sensors and it becomes 57 million potential series; add a raw path like `/v1/sensors/S-0042/status` instead of the template and every id is a route; add `request_id` and every request is a series that lives in memory until retention expires. Symptoms: Prometheus memory climbing (`prometheus_tsdb_head_series` in the millions), scrapes exceeding their timeout and being dropped, queries taking seconds, and the dashboard going blank during the incident you need it for. The offending metric is found with `topk(10, count by (__name__) ({__name__=~"beacon_.*"}))` and the offending label with `count(count by (sensor_id) (beacon_http_requests_total))`.' },
    { h: 'Bounding without losing the signal', body: 'The question is always "what decision did we want this label for?" If it was "which sensors are failing", that is a per-entity question and belongs in logs or a trace attribute, where one line costs one line. If it was "is one tenant hammering us", keep a bounded version: a tenant *class* (`free`, `standard`, `enterprise`), or a top-N computed elsewhere. For HTTP, the route label is the template from the router, not the request path; status is the code or the class (`2xx`). An allow-list in the instrumentation (`route = TEMPLATES.get(route, "other")`) makes the bound explicit so an unexpected value cannot create a series. After the fix, drop the bad series (`promtool tsdb` or wait for retention) and add a check: a rule that alerts when `prometheus_tsdb_head_series` grows past a budget.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'The metric that took the dashboard down', body: 'During a traffic spike the Beacon dashboard showed no data for the API for twenty minutes. Prometheus logs: `scrape timeout` for the API targets and memory at the Pod\'s limit. Query:\n\n```\ntopk(5, count by (__name__) ({__name__=~"beacon_.*"}))\nbeacon_ingest_records_total   1,842,117\nbeacon_http_requests_total        1,280\nbeacon_db_queries_total             96\n```\n\n`beacon_ingest_records_total` had been given a `sensor_id` label the week before, "so we can see which sensors are noisy". Each sensor was one series per replica; the spike added tens of thousands of new sensors from a test fleet. Fix: remove `sensor_id` from the counter (it keeps `source` with three values), log `sensor_id` on the ingest event, and answer "which sensors are noisy" with a log query. The dashboard recovered once the head series dropped after the bad series aged out. The evidence note recorded the before/after `prometheus_tsdb_head_series` and the lesson that the signal itself was never lost: it moved to the place where per-entity detail is affordable.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A histogram stores each latency and the quantile is exact.', right: 'It stores cumulative bucket counts; histogram_quantile interpolates inside a bucket, so precision depends on where the bucket edges are.' },
    { wrong: 'A label is cheap because it is just a string.', right: 'Every new label value multiplies series; Prometheus pays memory and query time per series. Unbounded values can take the monitoring down.' },
    { wrong: 'Removing a high-cardinality label loses the information.', right: 'It moves it: per-entity detail goes to logs or trace attributes, and the metric keeps a bounded class or a template.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: the SLO threshold is 300 ms and the buckets are 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1. Can the latency SLI "fraction under 300 ms" be computed exactly?', a: 'No. There is no bucket edge at 0.3; the best you get is an interpolation between the 0.25 and 0.5 buckets. Add a 0.3 bucket.' },
    { q: 'Why must `le` be kept when aggregating before `histogram_quantile`?', a: 'The function needs the bucket boundaries to locate the quantile; summing away `le` leaves a single number with no distribution.' },
    { q: 'Which query names the metric with the most series, and which the label responsible?', a: '`topk(10, count by (__name__) ({__name__=~"beacon_.*"}))` then `count(count by (<label>) (<metric>))` for candidate labels.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m11-04', moduleId: 'M11', title: 'SLIs, SLOs and error budgets', mins: 15,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m11-03'], prereqs: ['les-m11-03'], refs: ['T01', 'T14'],
  objective: 'Write Beacon\'s availability and latency SLIs as a numerator and denominator with explicit exclusions, set an SLO with a window, and compute the error budget and its remaining fraction on a fixture of request counts.',
  model: 'An **SLI** is a ratio: **good events ÷ valid events**, over a window. Both halves are definitions you must write down: what counts as *valid* (which requests are even in scope — and which are excluded, such as health checks or malformed client requests) and what counts as *good* (succeeded, or succeeded within a latency threshold). An **SLO** is a target for that ratio over a window (99.5% over 30 days). The **error budget** is the complement: 0.5% of valid events may be bad before the target is missed, and it is the number you spend on releases, experiments and incidents. The point of writing the numerator and denominator explicitly is that a wrong denominator makes a green SLO out of a failing service — the blind spot of a monitor that watches the wrong thing.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Valid events and explicit exclusions', body: 'For Beacon\'s status API, a *valid* event is an HTTP request to a `/v1/` route that reached the API. Written as PromQL, the denominator:\n\n```\nsum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total{route=~"/v1/.*"}[30d]))\n```\n\nExclusions, each with a reason: `/healthz` and `/metrics` (not user traffic; including them inflates the denominator with requests that always succeed); `4xx` responses are *valid* but *good* — the user sent a bad request and the service answered correctly — unless the 4xx is caused by us (an auth outage returning 401 to everyone), which is why a `401` spike is a separate alert, not an SLI change; requests that never reached the API (load balancer 502s) are *not visible* to this SLI and need an edge-measured one. Write the exclusions as the comment above the recording rule, so the next person can argue with them instead of discovering them.' },
    { h: 'Good events: availability and latency', body: 'Availability SLI: good = valid and not 5xx.\n\n```\nsum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total{route=~"/v1/.*", status!~"5.."}[30d]))\n  / sum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total{route=~"/v1/.*"}[30d]))\n```\n\nLatency SLI: good = valid and completed within 300 ms. The histogram makes this a bucket ratio, which is why 0.3 must be a bucket edge:\n\n```\nsum(rate(beacon_http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{route=~"/v1/.*", le="0.3"}[30d]))\n  / sum(rate(beacon_http_request_duration_seconds_count{route=~"/v1/.*"}[30d]))\n```\n\nBoth are "fraction of events that were good", which is why they can share one budget arithmetic. A percentile ("p95 < 300 ms") is a worse SLI: it is not a ratio of events, it cannot be summed across windows, and a small amount of traffic makes it jump. Record them as rules (`beacon:sli_availability:ratio_30d`) so dashboards and alerts use the same definition.' },
    { h: 'SLO, budget and the arithmetic', body: 'SLO: availability 99.5% over a rolling 30 days; latency 95% of valid requests under 300 ms over 30 days. Error budget = 1 − SLO = 0.5% of valid events. On a fixture:\n\n```\nvalid requests, 30d:      12,400,000\nbad (5xx), 30d:               31,000\nbudget = 0.005 × 12,400,000 = 62,000 bad requests allowed\nspent  = 31,000 / 62,000    = 50%  → 50% of the budget remains\nSLI    = 1 − 31,000/12,400,000 = 99.75%\n```\n\nThe budget is finite per window, so it answers operational questions: "can we do the risky migration this week" (how much remains), "was that incident significant" (it spent 20% of a month\'s budget in 40 minutes), "are we over-investing in reliability" (budget never spent). The window slides, so budget comes back as bad events age out of it. A **burn rate** is how fast budget is being spent relative to the rate that would exactly exhaust it at the window\'s end: burn 1 means on track to use 100% in 30 days; burn 10 means the whole month\'s budget gone in three days.' },
    { h: 'The blind spot', body: 'The S21 scenario in this module has a monitor that reports green while users fail. The mechanism is always a denominator or numerator that does not match what users experience: a probe that hits `/healthz` (which returns 200 from a process whose database connection is gone), an SLI computed from the API\'s own counters while the load balancer is returning 502s before the API, or an availability number that counts timeouts as "no response" and therefore neither good nor bad. The habit that catches it: for every SLI, name one failure mode users would notice and ask whether this ratio would move. If the answer is no, you either need a second SLI measured from a different vantage point or the definition is wrong.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'Computing Beacon\'s budget on the fixture file', body: 'The L22 fixture gives hourly counts for 30 days. The learner\'s script:\n\n```\nvalid = sum(h.requests for h in hours if h.route.startswith("/v1/"))        # 12,400,000\nbad   = sum(h.requests for h in hours if h.route.startswith("/v1/") and h.status >= 500)   # 31,000\nslow  = sum(h.over_300ms for h in hours if h.route.startswith("/v1/"))      # 540,000\nprint("availability SLI", 1 - bad/valid)             # 0.9975\nprint("latency SLI", 1 - slow/valid)                 # 0.9565\nprint("avail budget remaining", 1 - bad/(0.005*valid))      # 0.50\nprint("latency budget remaining", 1 - slow/(0.05*valid))    # 0.129\n```\n\nAvailability is comfortable; latency has 13% of its budget left with nine days to go in the window, which is a different conversation from the one the availability number would have started. A first version of the script had counted `/healthz` in `valid` and reported latency at 97.1% — green — because the 30 million fast health checks diluted the slow user requests. The exclusion was not pedantry; it was the difference between "fine" and "about to breach".' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'A 4xx is an error, so it counts against the SLO.', right: 'A correctly returned 4xx is the service working; it is valid and good. Only a 4xx we caused (auth outage) is a problem, and that needs its own signal.' },
    { wrong: 'p95 under 300 ms is a fine SLI.', right: 'A percentile is not a ratio of events: it cannot be budgeted, summed across windows or compared fairly at low traffic. "Fraction of requests under 300 ms" can.' },
    { wrong: 'Including health checks makes the SLI more complete.', right: 'It dilutes the denominator with traffic that always succeeds and hides user-facing failures. Exclude non-user traffic explicitly.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: 2,000,000 valid requests in the window, SLO 99.9%. How many bad requests can occur before the SLO is breached?', a: '0.001 × 2,000,000 = 2,000.' },
    { q: 'Write the latency SLI for "under 300 ms" as a bucket ratio and name the precondition on the histogram.', a: '`sum(rate(..._bucket{le="0.3"}[w])) / sum(rate(..._count[w]))`; 0.3 must be one of the bucket boundaries.' },
    { q: 'An SLI built from API counters shows 99.9% while the load balancer returns 502 to half of users. Which half of the ratio is wrong?', a: 'The denominator: requests that never reached the API are not counted as valid, so the failures are invisible. Measure from the edge as well.' },
    { q: 'What does a burn rate of 6 mean over a 30-day window?', a: 'Budget is being consumed six times faster than the on-track pace: the whole month\'s budget would be gone in five days.' }
  ]
});

LX.sreLessons.push({
  id: 'les-m11-05', moduleId: 'M11', title: 'Alerting on user impact', mins: 16,
  objectiveIds: ['obj-m11-04'], prereqs: ['les-m11-04'], refs: ['T01', 'T14'],
  objective: 'Write alert rules that page on sustained error-budget burn using multiple windows, clear when the burn stops, treat missing data as its own alert rather than as health, and test them with promtool fixtures instead of waiting for an outage.',
  model: 'Alert on **symptoms users feel**, measured as **burn rate** against the error budget, not on causes (CPU, restarts, a queue depth) that may or may not hurt anyone. A **burn-rate alert** compares the recent bad-event ratio to the budget: `error_ratio > burn × (1 − SLO)`. Using **two windows** — a long one (so a brief blip does not page) and a short one (so the alert *clears* promptly once the burn stops) — gives a page that fires on sustained impact and resolves honestly. A ratio with no traffic is `NaN`, and a target that stopped being scraped produces *no series at all*; neither is "healthy", and `absent()` is how you alert on silence. Rules are code: **promtool** unit tests feed synthetic series and assert exactly which alerts fire at which minute.',
  sections: [
    { h: 'Why burn rate, and the multi-window shape', body: 'A rule like `error_ratio > 0.01 for 5m` fires on a two-minute blip that spent nothing meaningful and goes quiet during a slow leak that empties the budget over a week. Burn rate fixes both: pick the burn that justifies a page. For a 30-day, 99.5% SLO, burn 14.4 sustained over one hour spends 2% of the month\'s budget in that hour — worth waking someone. Burn 6 over six hours spends 5% — a ticket for the morning. Burn 1 over three days is "on track to breach" — review. Each gets a long window and a short window (one twelfth of the long one is the usual choice):\n\n```\n- alert: BeaconAvailabilityBurnFast\n  expr: |\n    (beacon:error_ratio:1h  > (14.4 * 0.005))\n    and\n    (beacon:error_ratio:5m  > (14.4 * 0.005))\n  for: 2m\n  labels: { severity: page }\n  annotations: { summary: "Beacon burning 30d error budget at >14.4x over 1h (and still burning over 5m)" }\n```\n\nThe long window proves it is sustained; the short window proves it is *still* happening, so the alert resolves within minutes of recovery instead of staying red for an hour while the 1h average drains.' },
    { h: 'Clearing correctly and avoiding flap', body: 'An alert is active while its expression returns a result and has been for `for:`; it resolves when the expression returns nothing. Three things keep that honest. Thresholds with no hysteresis flap when a value hovers near them; the short window plus `for:` dampens this, and a rule that pages should rarely need a `for:` above a few minutes. A recording rule for the ratio (`beacon:error_ratio:5m`) keeps the alert expression readable and lets the dashboard show exactly what the alert sees. And every page carries a runbook link and the two numbers that justified it; an alert that says "something is wrong" sends the responder to the dashboard to re-derive what the rule already knew.' },
    { h: 'No data is not good news', body: 'If the API stops being scraped (Pod gone, NetworkPolicy blocking the scrape, Service selector broken), `beacon_http_requests_total` has no fresh samples; `rate()` over it returns an empty result, the ratio returns nothing, and a burn alert built on it *resolves*. The dashboard shows a gap that looks like a quiet night. Separately, a division with zero traffic produces `NaN`, which fails every comparison and also looks like "no alert". Cover both explicitly:\n\n```\n- alert: BeaconMetricsAbsent\n  expr: absent(up{job="beacon-api"}) or (up{job="beacon-api"} == 0)\n  for: 3m\n  labels: { severity: page }\n- alert: BeaconNoTraffic\n  expr: sum(rate(beacon_http_requests_total{route=~"/v1/.*"}[10m])) == 0\n  for: 10m\n  labels: { severity: ticket }\n```\n\n`absent()` returns a series with value 1 when its argument matches nothing, which is the only way to alert on silence. Whether zero traffic is a page depends on the service: for Beacon, sensors report continuously, so zero inbound traffic at any hour is itself a symptom.' },
    { h: 'Testing rules with promtool', body: 'Waiting for an incident to find out whether an alert fires is a test strategy with a very long feedback loop. `promtool test rules` loads a rules file, feeds synthetic series and asserts alerts at a given evaluation time:\n\n```\nrule_files: [beacon-alerts.yaml]\nevaluation_interval: 1m\ntests:\n- interval: 1m\n  input_series:\n  - series: \'beacon_http_requests_total{route="/v1/status",status="200"}\'\n    values: \'0+100x120\'           # 100 per minute for 2h\n  - series: \'beacon_http_requests_total{route="/v1/status",status="500"}\'\n    values: \'0+0x60 0+20x60\'      # none for 1h, then 20/min (16.7% errors)\n  alert_rule_test:\n  - eval_time: 61m\n    alertname: BeaconAvailabilityBurnFast\n    exp_alerts: []                # not yet sustained over 1h\n  - eval_time: 110m\n    alertname: BeaconAvailabilityBurnFast\n    exp_alerts:\n    - exp_labels: { severity: page, alertname: BeaconAvailabilityBurnFast }\n```\n\n`promtool check rules beacon-alerts.yaml` catches syntax; the unit test catches the logic: fires when it should, stays quiet before it should, and — with a third case where the error series stops — resolves. The absent rule gets its own test with no `up` series at all. Run both in the pipeline as a gate, because a broken alert rule is a silent failure with no user-visible symptom until it matters.' }
  ],
  example: { h: 'The alert that never fired, and the one that never cleared', body: 'Two findings from the S22 review of Beacon\'s alerts. First, `BeaconErrorRate: sum(rate(...{status=~"5.."}[5m])) / sum(rate(...[5m])) > 0.05` did not fire during a 25-minute outage in which the API Pods were OOM-killed and restarting: with no Pods ready, the target was down, `rate()` had no samples, the ratio was empty, and the rule was silent. The fix was the `absent(up{job="beacon-api"})` page plus a burn-rate rule evaluated on a recording rule that counts missing samples as nothing rather than nothing-wrong. Second, `BeaconLatencyHigh: ... [1h] > 0.3` stayed firing for 55 minutes after a 5-minute database stall, because a single 1h window needs the slow samples to age out; replacing it with the 1h-and-5m pair made it resolve four minutes after the stall ended. Both rules now have promtool tests: one with the `up` series missing, one with a 5-minute spike that must fire and then clear by minute 12.' },
  misconceptions: [
    { wrong: 'If the error-rate alert is quiet, errors are low.', right: 'It may be quiet because there is no data: a target that is down or blocked produces no samples and an empty ratio. Alert on absence separately.' },
    { wrong: 'A longer window makes an alert better.', right: 'Longer windows resist blips but take as long to clear as to fire. Pair a long window (sustained) with a short one (still happening).' },
    { wrong: 'Alert on CPU, restarts and queue depth so we catch problems early.', right: 'Those are causes that may or may not hurt users; they page people for nothing and miss impacts with other causes. Page on burn, ticket on causes.' }
  ],
  recall: [
    { q: 'Predict: the API Deployment is scaled to zero by mistake. What does a plain `error_ratio > 0.05` alert do, and what should fire?', a: 'Nothing: no samples, empty ratio, the alert stays resolved. `absent(up{job="beacon-api"})` (or `up == 0`) should page.' },
    { q: 'For a 30-day 99.5% SLO, what error ratio does a 14.4× burn correspond to, and why pair a 1h window with a 5m one?', a: '14.4 × 0.005 = 7.2% of requests bad. The 1h window proves it is sustained; the 5m window makes the alert clear soon after the burn stops.' },
    { q: 'Which promtool command checks that a rule fires at minute 110 and not at minute 61 given synthetic series?', a: '`promtool test rules <test-file>` with `input_series` and `alert_rule_test` entries at those `eval_time`s; `promtool check rules` only checks syntax.' }
  ]
});

